### acquisitions.md  [docs]
+ # Acquisitions — Buying a Company
+ Most small acquisitions fail on integration, not on price. Decide what you are buying before you decide what it is worth, because the answer determines everything downstream — the price, the structure, and whether the thing you bought still exists in a year.
+ ## What Are You Actually Buying
+ | Type | You are buying | Price logic | The thing that kills it |
+ |---|---|---|---|
+ | Acquihire | A team that already works together | Per-engineer convention plus retention packages; verify against current market comps, they move fast | The team leaves at the end of the retention period; you bought a year |
+ | Product / tuck-in | Shipped code and a roadmap shortcut | Build-cost avoided, discounted for integration reality | Rewriting it anyway within 18 months |
+ | Customers / book of business | Revenue and logos | Multiple of the revenue that survives the transition | Churn at renewal when their champion sees a new owner |
+ | Technology / IP | A capability you cannot build in time | Time-to-market value | The one engineer who understands it leaves |
+ | Competitor | Removing price pressure, consolidating share | Synergy, market structure | Two overlapping products, neither retired, both maintained |
+ | Distribution | Their channel or market access | Contribution margin the channel can carry | The channel was the founder's relationships |
+ Write the answer in one sentence before opening negotiations. "We are buying eight engineers who ship in our stack" and "we are buying a product line" lead to completely different deals with the same company.
+ ## Before The Offer
+ - Build-buy-partner honestly: what would this cost to build, in months, with the team you actually have and their current commitments? Most buy cases are really "we would never prioritize it", which is a valid reason, stated honestly.
+ - Talk to their customers before you talk about price. Whether the revenue survives an ownership change is knowable in five calls.
+ - Understand why they are selling. Founders exhausted, out of runway, or facing a competitor's move each imply a different price and a different post-close reality.
+ - Model the fully loaded cost: purchase price + retention packages + integration engineering + the quarter of distraction across your own team. Integration is routinely under-budgeted because nobody owns it: staff it as a real project with a named owner, a dated end, and engineering capacity carved out of the roadmap before you sign, not after.
+ ## Diligence That Matters at This Size
+ - **Cap table and clean title**: every share, option, SAFE, note, and side letter accounted for. IP assigned from every founder, employee, and contractor — an unassigned contractor contribution is the most common deal-blocker in small acquisitions (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance).
+ - **Code and license audit**: open-source licenses (copyleft in a commercial product is a real problem), third-party dependencies, and whether anything was built for a client who owns it.
+ - **Customer contracts**: change-of-control clauses, assignment rights, termination-on-acquisition, MFN and price commitments you inherit.
+ - **Employment**: who has an offer letter versus a contractor agreement, non-competes, visa status of key people, accrued and unpaid comp.
+ - **Liabilities**: unpaid taxes (payroll and sales tax nexus), pending disputes, deferred revenue you must deliver against.
+ - **The key-person question**: which two or three people must stay for this to work? Their retention terms are part of the deal, not an HR detail after it.
+ ## Structure
+ - **Price is a package**: cash at close, stock, retention pool, escrow or holdback, earnout. Small-deal convention holds 10-15% in escrow for 12-18 months against reps and warranties; negotiate the cap and survival period, not just the amount.
+ - **Earnouts**: assume the earnout does not pay. Price the deal on the cash at close, and treat the earnout as an alignment device with metrics that survive your own integration decisions. An earnout tied to a metric you will change is a lawsuit with a schedule.
+ - **Retention**: separate purchase consideration from retention comp explicitly, vesting over 2-3 years. Sellers will push for value at close; the whole point is what happens after.
+ - **Deal structure has tax consequences for the sellers** that materially change the price they need (asset versus stock purchase, cash versus stock). Bring tax counsel in before you name a number, or you will renegotiate.
+ ## Integration — Decide Before Signing
+ The three questions to answer in writing before close, because after close everyone has an opinion:
+ 1. **Product**: sunset, maintain, or merge — with a date. "We'll decide later" means maintaining two products forever.
+ 2. **People**: who reports to whom on day one, which of their leaders keeps a leadership role, and where the team physically and organizationally sits.
+ 3. **Brand and customers**: what the customers of the acquired product are told, by whom, in the first 48 hours.
+ - Name one integration owner from your side, full-time, senior, for at least a quarter. Part-time integration ownership is why acquisitions drift.
+ - Day-one basics: systems access, payroll, benefits, email, and a clear statement about what is not changing. Anxiety in the first week produces the departures you spent the retention pool to prevent.
+ - Compensation and level mapping will be discovered by the acquired team within days. Decide it deliberately and explain the logic, or you import a permanent grievance.
+ - Kill the acquired roadmap explicitly if you are killing it. The team keeping faith with a dead roadmap is the most common quiet failure.
+ ## Traps
+ | Trap | Why it fails | Do instead |
+ |---|---|---|
+ | Buying revenue you cannot retain | Renewals arrive and the customers were the founder's relationships | Model on post-transition revenue, structure with an earnout tied to retention |
+ | No integration owner | Everyone assumes someone else is doing it; six months pass | One named full-time owner before signing |
+ | Culture "we'll figure out later" | Two operating systems in one company; the acquired team leaves in year two | Decide the operating model pre-close and say it plainly |
+ | Announcing before the team is told | Their employees learn from press; retention collapses on day one | Their team hears first (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: communication) |
+ | CEO runs the deal alone | Diligence gaps, and the whole exec team is a spectator to something they must execute | Assign functional diligence owners, keep the board informed early (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: board) |
+ | Acquiring to fix a strategy problem | You now have two unclear strategies and one integration project | Fix the strategy first (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: strategy) |

### board.md  [docs]
- - 1 week before: full deck and materials sent. Day of: zero new information.
- Template: one-liner on company state · 3-5 metrics with trend · wins · challenges (honest) · asks (specific: intros, references, expertise) · what to watch next month.
- **Cash running low**: raise it while options exist — at the 12-18 month runway mark (zones in `finance.md`), not inside 6. Show cut scenarios ("cut X, runway becomes Y"), propose one plan, and ask directly about bridge appetite.
- Board structure is negotiated at the term sheet, never after (→ `fundraising.md`). The independent seat is your tiebreaker — recruit it yourself, deliberately, before you need the vote.
+ - 5-7 days before: full deck and materials sent (the packet lead time, canonical below). Day of: zero new information.
+ Template: one-liner on company state · the same five metrics with trend (the CEO page — → SKILL.md Quick Reference: metrics) · wins · challenges (honest) · asks (specific: intros, references, expertise) · what to watch next month.
+ **Cash running low**: raise it while options exist — no later than `runway_alarm_months` (default 12), the band boundary defined in the canonical zone table (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: finance). Show cut scenarios ("cut X, runway becomes Y"), propose one plan, and ask directly about bridge appetite.
+ Board structure is negotiated at the term sheet, never after (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: fundraising). The independent seat is your tiebreaker — recruit it yourself, deliberately, before you need the vote.
+ - Observers have information rights, not votes or duties. Write down what they receive; an undefined observer becomes a shadow director with none of the accountability (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance).
+ ## The Board Packet
+ Sent 5-7 days ahead — the canonical lead time for any board or committee material — and read before the room. What belongs in it: a one-page CEO letter with the honest headline, the same five metrics every time with identical definitions (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: metrics), financials against plan with variance explanations, an updated cash and runway view, progress on the three priorities including the one that is behind, a risk page (concentration, key-person, legal, covenant), and the two or three strategic questions you want their brains on — framed as questions with your recommendation attached.
+ What does not belong: new numbers on the day, unlabeled definition changes, or a metric that appears once because it was good this quarter. A board that catches one undisclosed definition change treats every subsequent number as suspect.
+ ## A Difficult Director
+ Diagnose before reacting; the treatments differ:
+ | Pattern | What is usually happening | Move |
+ |---|---|---|
+ | Relitigates settled decisions | They lost the argument and never felt heard | Acknowledge the disagreement explicitly, restate the falsifier that would reopen it (SKILL.md rule 9) |
+ | Operates in your company's day-to-day | Fund pressure, or a genuine capability gap they see | Give them a scoped, real assignment; ambiguity invites freelancing |
+ | Consistently unprepared | The company is not a priority in their portfolio | Shorter deck, direct ask, and stop spending pre-wire time proportional to their influence |
+ | Pushes their fund's outcome over the company's | A real conflict of interest | Name it privately, in the language of duty, and involve the independent (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance) |
+ | Undermines you with other directors | Loss of confidence, usually not new | Direct conversation first; then the chair or lead investor. Do not let it be discovered at a vote |
+ Escalation path when it does not resolve: the lead director or chair, then a board-level conversation with the independent as broker. A director cannot usually be removed except by whoever holds the right to that seat — which is why who appoints the independent is negotiated at the term sheet, not after (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: fundraising).
+ ## Board Health
+ - Ask each director annually what would make the board more useful to you, and what you should do differently. Two questions, individually, off the record.
+ - The board evaluates the CEO at least annually with written criteria agreed in advance. A CEO who does not ask for this ends up being evaluated informally, in a closed session, against criteria they never saw (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: succession).
+ - Between meetings, use written consents for routine approvals so option grants and hires do not wait a quarter (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance).
+ - The board is at its most useful on three things: the top team, capital allocation, and the hard call you are avoiding. Bring those and the meeting justifies itself.

### ceo-operating-system.md  [docs]
+ # The CEO's Own Operating System
+ Your calendar is your strategy, observed. Porter and Nohria's HBR CEO time study found meetings consume roughly 70% of CEO hours — drift is the default state, and deep work exists only if it is defended like a board meeting.
+ ## The Calendar
+ | Block | Rule |
+ |-------|------|
+ | Deep work (4h/week minimum) | Strategy, writing, thinking. Scheduled first, moved last |
+ | 1:1s | Weekly with directs, monthly skip-levels (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: people) |
+ | External | Investors, customers, partners, recruiting — the parts only the CEO can do (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: customers) |
+ | All-hands | Monthly minimum, live (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: communication) |
+ | Board | Prep and meetings on the `board_cadence` rhythm, pre-wires scheduled with the meeting |
+ | Buffer | 15 minutes between blocks; a packed calendar makes every overrun cascade into the evening |
+ **Monthly calendar audit**: score the last four weeks against your three priorities and the three undelegable jobs (SKILL.md rule 1). A priority with near-zero hours is a stated priority, not a real one — fix the calendar or fix the priority list. Track a single number: the share of hours that went to the three priorities. Under 50% is a diagnosis.
+ Two structural defenses that work: theme your days (external days versus internal days, so context switching happens weekly rather than hourly), and put a standing hold where the deep work goes before the quarter fills.
+ ## Running Meetings as Production
+ Meetings are the medium of managerial work (Grove, *High Output Management*) — run them as output, not ceremony.
+ **Weekly exec staff, 60-90 min**: metrics against plan, exceptions only (15) → decisions needed, pre-read circulated (30) → cross-functional blockers (15) → announcements (10). Decisions in the middle when energy is highest. Anything that could be a document is sent as a document.
+ - Every recurring meeting has a written purpose, an owner, and a kill date at which it must justify itself. Meetings never die of natural causes.
+ - No decision without a named decider in the invite (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: decisions). A meeting to "discuss" with no decider produces a second meeting.
+ - Notes and decisions published within 24 hours, including for those absent. The absent person acting on old information is a coordination failure you caused.
+ - Quarterly offsite, 1-2 days, no laptops: strategy review, team dynamics, next-quarter planning.
+ ## Your Decision Queue
+ - Batch the decisions that need you. A CEO who decides continuously is interruptible continuously, and neither the decisions nor the thinking get done well.
+ - Anything you decide the same way three times becomes a written default with an owner, and leaves your queue permanently.
+ - Escalations arrive as options plus a recommendation, or they go back. An open question escalated is delegation upward, and accepting it teaches the org to do it (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: decisions).
+ - The delegation test per task, not per person (Grove's task-relevant maturity): has this person done this before, here? If yes, they own it including the mistakes. If no, coach through the first one and then hand it over.
+ - Accept 80% of your standard from an owner over 100% from yourself. At any real scale that trade is not close (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: scaling).
+ ## Your Information Diet
+ You will be told what people think you want to hear, at increasing rates as the company grows. Counter it deliberately:
+ - Skip-levels monthly, front-line exposure monthly: support tickets, sales calls, or a shift with the delivery team.
+ - Read raw material, not summaries: three lost-deal notes, three churn reasons, three support threads (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: metrics, customers).
+ - Reward the person who brings you the bad news, publicly, the first time it happens. The whole org is watching what happened to them.
+ - Keep two or three people who will tell you that you are wrong, and make sure at least one does not work for you.
+ - Notice the questions nobody asks you anymore. That is the shape of what people have decided not to raise.
+ ## Executive Exits (mechanics)
+ The decision criteria live under people (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference); the choreography is here because it is a CEO operation.
+ - Decide, then act within days. Tell the board chair before you tell the exec.
+ - The conversation is short and final: the decision, the terms, the timing, the story. Not a debate, not a performance review.
+ - Announce internally the same day, with who owns the function now — an announcement without a named successor or interim produces a week of paralysis and often a second departure.
+ - Say what is true and kind, and nothing more: "X is leaving, Y owns the function, here is the plan." Do not litigate the reasons in public; the team fills gaps with the most generous interpretation only if you have earned it.
+ - Transfer the relationships (customers, candidates, partners) yourself in the first week.
+ ## Self-Management
+ | Risk | Mitigation |
+ |------|------------|
+ | Burnout | Sleep, exercise, real vacations — scheduled a quarter ahead, not aspirational |
+ | Isolation | External confidants and a CEO peer group; your exec team cannot be your therapist and your co-founder is not neutral |
+ | Decision fatigue | Defaults and delegation for the trivial; spend judgment where it compounds |
+ | Echo chamber | The information diet above, enforced monthly |
+ | Identity fusion | The company's results are not your worth; CEOs who cannot separate them cannot make the exit or shutdown call cleanly (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: shutdown) |
+ | Imposter syndrome | Normal. The job is hard for everyone doing it honestly |
+ The team calibrates to your demeanor before your words: anxiety spreads, calm spreads. Panic privately, with your confidants — never in the all-hands (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: crisis).
+ Take a real two-week vacation annually, with a named acting decision-maker. It is a systems test: what breaks reveals your single points of failure, and what does not break is what you can finally stop carrying (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: succession).

### cofounders.md  [docs]
+ # Co-Founders — Equity, Roles, Conflict, Departure
+ Co-founder conflict is one of the most common ways early companies die, and it almost always traces back to three conversations that were never had: who decides, who owns what, and what happens if one of us leaves.
+ ## Equity Splits
+ - Split for the future, not the past. Equity pays for the next five years of work and risk; the person who had the idea is being compensated for a month.
+ - Near-equal splits are the norm among successful teams and the argument you have about the last 5% is a preview of every future argument. A 60/40 that both parties genuinely accept beats a 50/50 that one resents.
+ - If a split has to be unequal, write the reason in one sentence you would both read aloud at a board meeting. Reasons that survive: full-time versus part-time, capital contributed, a genuinely irreplaceable asset. Reasons that do not: age, who spoke first, who is louder.
+ - **Dynamic splits are for pre-formation only.** Once real work starts, an unresolved split poisons every decision and blocks any priced round.
+ - Do not leave equity unallocated "to decide later". Later is when you have the least leverage and the most emotion.
+ ## The Mechanics You Cannot Skip
+ | Instrument | Why it exists | The failure it prevents |
+ |---|---|---|
+ |---|---|---|
+ | Vesting on founder shares, 4 years with a 1-year cliff | Equity is earned over time | A co-founder leaves at month 7 holding 40% forever — the "dead equity" that kills your next round |
+ | 83(b) election, filed within 30 days of the grant (US) | Taxes the shares at grant value | A tax bill on vesting appreciation with no cash and no way to fix it — the 30-day window has no exception |
+ | IP assignment from every founder | The company owns what you built | Code written before incorporation belonging to someone personally, or to their old employer |
+ | Written role and decision split | Names one decider per domain | Two people deciding everything, so nothing gets decided fast |
+ | Acceleration terms defined at the start | Sets what a change of control does | Discovering in an acquisition that a departed founder's terms block the deal (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: exit) |
+ If founder shares were issued without vesting, fix it now by agreement while everyone is friendly. Investors will require it anyway, and the conversation is much worse when there is money on the table.
+ ## Roles and Who Decides
+ - One CEO. Co-CEOs work in a handful of famous cases and fail quietly in most others: employees route to whoever gives the answer they want, and the ambiguity becomes visible to the board at exactly the wrong moment.
+ - Split by domain, not by seniority, and write it down: each domain has one owner whose decision stands even when the other disagrees.
+ - Define the small set of decisions requiring both: fundraising terms, hiring or firing an exec, changing the strategy, spending above a threshold. Everything else has one owner.
+ - Deadlock rule agreed in advance: the CEO decides, or a named board member breaks the tie. Agreeing this while calm is the entire point.
+ - Non-CEO founders need a real job with real outcomes. "Founder" is not a role; a founder without a domain becomes a floating opinion, which is corrosive to every exec you hire (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: people).
+ ## Maintenance
+ - Weekly 1:1 between founders, protected, agenda-free enough for the hard thing to surface. Most conflicts are six months of unsaid small things.
+ - Quarterly relationship review, separate from any business review: what is working, what is not, whether the role split still matches the company's stage.
+ - Never disagree in front of the team about a decision already made — the org learns which founder to lobby, and you get factions (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: decisions).
+ - Never let one founder be the only one talking to the board. A board that only hears one founder cannot help with the founder problem when it arrives.
+ - Say the resentment early, in the 1:1, when it is still a small correction. The version at month eighteen comes with a lawyer.
+ ## When It Is Not Working
+ Diagnose which one it is, because the treatments are different:
+ | Pattern | Underlying issue | Move |
+ | Chronic disagreement on direction | Different companies in their heads | Write both strategies down, decide once, formally (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: strategy) |
+ | One is not delivering | Capability or effort mismatch | Same conversation you would have with any exec, held earlier than feels comfortable |
+ | One has outgrown the role | The company scaled past them | Role change with dignity while equity stays earned (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: scaling) |
+ | Trust is gone | Broken commitments or hidden information | Usually terminal; get to a clean separation quickly |
+ | Both are miserable but neither will say it | Sunk cost | Third party — board member, coach — in the room |
+ Mediation with an experienced outside party is worth trying once, early. It fails when the real answer is already known and one side is avoiding saying it.
+ ## Separation
+ 1. Decide it properly and fast. A slow founder divorce consumes the whole exec team's attention and every employee can see it.
+ 2. Vested equity is earned and stays. Unvested does not vest as a kindness — it becomes dead equity that your next investor will make you clean up, and you will not be able to.
+ 3. Agree the terms in one document: equity treatment, board seat, title, information rights, severance, non-disparagement, and who says what. Independent counsel for both sides is cheaper than the alternative.
+ 4. Board seat handling matters more than the title: a departed founder holding a board seat can block financings for years (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance).
+ 5. Tell the team promptly with a clean, non-blaming story you can both repeat, and tell investors before they hear it (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: communication). The story you agree on is the one that travels for the next decade.
+ 6. Expect the remaining founder's workload and loneliness to spike. Line up a peer group or coach before the departure, not after (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: ceo-operating-system).

### communication.md  [docs]
+ # Communication — All-Hands, Memos, Bad News, Press
+ The CEO's output is decisions and language. A decision the company misunderstands was not made.
+ ## The Sequencing Rule
+ Order every announcement by who is most damaged by hearing it secondhand:
+ 1. The people directly affected (individually, in advance where legally possible)
+ 2. The exec team and managers, with the script and the FAQ, before they are asked
+ 3. The whole company
+ 4. Customers with a relationship at stake, by a human, same day
+ 5. Investors and board — pre-wired earlier for anything consequential (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: board)
+ 6. Press, if at all
+ Compress the whole sequence into one day for anything leakable. A layoff, a departure, or an acquisition that takes two days to communicate has already been communicated for you, badly (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: layoffs).
+ ## Writing the Memo
+ Deliverable format follows `deliverable_style`; the structure does not change.
+ - Lead with the decision in the first two sentences. Context that precedes the point reads as a build-up to bad news and everyone skips to the end anyway.
+ - Structure: decision → why now → what changes for you → what does not change → what happens next and when → who to ask.
+ - One page for a decision, two for a strategy change. If it needs five, the thinking is not finished.
+ - Name the trade-off you accepted. A memo that presents a hard call as costless tells the smart people on your team you either did not see the cost or are hiding it.
+ - Delete every hedge that survives a re-read: "we're exploring", "at this time", "a number of". Hedges are what people quote back when they lose trust.
+ - Write the sentence you would not want screenshotted, then decide whether it is true. If it is true and necessary, keep it and own it. If it is neither, the whole paragraph goes.
+ - Say the number. "Significant growth" is unreadable; "ARR grew from 4.2M to 6.1M" is a fact people can carry.
+ ## All-Hands
+ Monthly minimum, live, with the CEO present. Structure that survives contact:
+ | Block | Minutes | Content |
+ |---|---|---|
+ |---|---|---|
+ | Where we are | 5 | The same five metrics every month, same definitions (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: metrics) |
+ | Progress against the three priorities | 10 | Including the one that is behind |
+ | The decision or theme of the month | 10 | One thing, explained properly |
+ | Recognition | 5 | Specific work, named people, tied to a priority |
+ | Q&A | 20+ | Submitted in advance and live |
+ - Repeat the strategy until you are sick of saying it — that is roughly when the org has heard it once. A message delivered a single time was mentioned, not communicated.
+ - Answer the submitted-question list including the uncomfortable ones. Skipping one visible question costs more credibility than a bad answer to it.
+ - "I don't know" plus "here is when I will know" is a complete answer. Inventing an answer to look decisive is how CEOs get caught.
+ - Never announce a decision at an all-hands that any affected person is hearing for the first time.
+ - Remote and asynchronous: post the recording and a written summary within 24 hours; the summary is what actually spreads, so write it deliberately rather than auto-transcribing.
+ ## Bad News
+ Four parts, in this order: what happened · what it means for you · what we are doing about it · what I need from you. Nothing else belongs in the message.
+ - Deliver it early and complete. Drip-feeding bad news makes every subsequent update suspect, and the team spends the interval speculating instead of working.
+ - Own the decision in the first person. "The board decided" is true and useless — you were in the room, and passive voice reads as evasion.
+ - No forced optimism in the same breath. Let it be bad, then be specific about the response.
+ - Anticipate the three questions the room actually has (usually: am I safe, is the company safe, what changed about my work) and answer them unprompted.
+ - If you cannot yet say something, say that you cannot and when you will be able to. "I can't discuss that until Thursday" is respected; silence is not.
+ ## Hostile Q&A
+ - Repeat the question as asked, not as you wish it had been asked. Softening it in the repeat is visible to everyone.
+ - Answer the question that was asked before the one you prepared for.
+ - Do not punish the asker, ever, including with tone. The room is calibrating whether questions are safe here, and one flinch closes the channel for a year.
+ - Take the personal or personnel-specific one offline, on the record, with a commitment to a time.
+ - When you were wrong, say the wrong decision out loud and what you changed. It costs one uncomfortable minute and buys a year of candor.
+ ## Press and Public
+ - No off-the-record conversation with a journalist you do not have a track record with. "Off the record" is a norm, not a contract, and the rules differ per outlet.
+ - Decide what you would be comfortable seeing as the headline, then say only that. Journalists write from the most interesting sentence, not the main one.
+ - Correct facts, not framing (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: crisis).
+ - Your employees read everything you publish. A public claim your own team knows is inflated costs you internal credibility first.
+ - Never comment on a rival, a lawsuit in progress, or an unclosed deal. "We don't comment on that" is a complete answer and creates no story.
+ ## Investor and Board Updates
+ Monthly, especially when it is hard — the first believers in your next round are the investors who watched you report bad months honestly (format and template → SKILL.md Quick Reference: board).
+ ## Traps
+ | Trap | Why it fails | Do instead |
+ | Announcing before the FAQ exists | Managers improvise; six versions of the policy exist by lunch | Script and FAQ to managers first |
+ | Optimism inflation | Every update sounds good; the team stops listening and stops reporting risk upward | Report the behind item first, every time |
+ | Delegating the hard message | The team reads absence as cowardice, and the message loses its authority | CEO delivers company-level bad news personally |
+ | Writing to be quoted, not understood | Slogans that survive the meeting but decide nothing | Plain language, one decision per message |
+ | Different story to different audiences | Employees, customers, and investors talk to each other | One set of facts; adjust depth, never content |
+ | Silence during uncertainty | The rumor fills it, and the rumor is worse | Publish the cadence: "update every Friday until this resolves" |

### competition.md  [docs]
+ # Competition — Responding Without Losing the Plot
+ Most competitive damage is self-inflicted: a roadmap bent by a rival's press release, a price cut that resets the market, an org that starts measuring itself against the wrong company.
+ ## Classify the Threat Before Responding
+ | Event | What it usually means | Default response |
+ |---|---|---|
+ | Rival raises a big round | Capital, not product-market fit; expect noise for two quarters | None publicly. Expect their spend in your channels and their recruiters in your inbox |
+ | Rival ships your feature | Your differentiation was a feature, not a position | Check win rates in 30 days, not the roadmap in 30 minutes |
+ | Rival undercuts on price | They are buying logos, often below their own cost | Hold price, sharpen value, add a term or volume concession instead (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: pricing) |
+ | Rival wins a deal you expected | One data point until it is three | Loss review with the buyer, not with your rep |
+ | Incumbent bundles a free version | The dangerous one: distribution beats features | Move to where the bundle cannot follow (depth, workflow, segment) |
+ | New entrant counter-positioned against you | They can do something you structurally cannot copy without hurting your business | Name it honestly; incumbents lose here by pretending it is nothing (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: strategy) |
+ | Rival poaches your staff | Comp gap, or your people have no growth path | Fix the cause; counteroffers buy 6 months and teach the wrong lesson (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: people) |
+ | FUD about you in deals | You are on their battlecard, which means you are winning some | Arm sales with a factual, unemotional rebuttal; never respond in public |
+ | Rival shuts down or is acquired | Their customers are in play for roughly one quarter | Named migration offer within two weeks, with a human doing the migration |
+ Default when unclassified: gather three data points from lost deals before changing anything. A competitor's announcement is marketing; your win rate is data.
+ ## The Price-War Arithmetic
+ Before matching a discount, compute the volume it demands. Extra unit volume needed to hold gross profit = d / (m − d), where m is gross margin and d is the price cut, both as fractions of price.
+ - 70% margin, 10% cut → 0.10/0.60 = 16.7% more volume just to break even.
+ - 70% margin, 20% cut → 0.20/0.50 = 40% more volume.
+ - 40% margin, 20% cut → 0.20/0.20 = 100% more volume — you must double the business.
+ Price cuts are also close to irreversible: customers ratchet down easily and up painfully, and public list-price cuts reset the market's expectation permanently. Concede term length, volume commitment, or scope before you concede price.
+ ## Win/Loss That Is Actually Useful
+ - Interview the buyer, not the seller. Reps report price; buyers report risk, timing, and the internal champion who lost an argument.
+ - Do it within two weeks of the decision, by someone who was not on the deal. Losses go cold and get retold as price.
+ - Ask: who else was in it, what were the two finalists' differences, what would have had to be true for us to win, who decided.
+ - Segment the losses. Losing every deal in one vertical is a positioning problem; losing evenly is a product or price problem.
+ - The CEO reads loss notes directly at least monthly. Summarized loss data always arrives flattering.
+ ## Competitive Intelligence Without Paranoia
+ Legitimate and sufficient: their pricing page and changelog history, job postings (the roadmap in plain sight — a "sales engineer, healthcare" posting names their next segment), customer reviews, conference talks, their investors' stated theses, and your own lost deals. Track four things per rival — positioning, price, their best segment, and their weakness — on one page, reviewed quarterly.
+ Out of bounds and career-ending: misrepresenting yourself to get a demo or documents, hiring someone to bring confidential material, or inducing a breach of their NDA. Also worthless: a 40-tab battlecard nobody reads.
+ ## Choosing Not To Compete
+ - Concede segments deliberately. Where they are structurally advantaged, losing cheaply beats losing expensively — and publishing an honest "not for you, try them" wins the deals you should win (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: strategy).
+ - Never let a competitor set your roadmap: a feature is worth building because customers pay for it, not because a rival shipped it. Track how many roadmap items exist only because of a competitor; more than one in five means they are running your product team.
+ - Measure against your own customers' alternatives, including doing nothing in a spreadsheet — that is the real incumbent in most categories.
+ - Do not mention competitors by name in your own marketing. It gives them free distribution and reads as insecurity to anyone who had not heard of them.
+ ## The Board Conversation
+ Boards over-index on competitors because it is the easiest thing to have an opinion about. Come with the data — win rate by segment over three quarters, price realization, pipeline sourced against theirs — and a stated position: where you win, where you concede, and what would change that. A board given no position will supply one, and it will be a feature request.

### crisis.md  [docs]
+ # Crisis — The First 24 Hours, By Type
+ A crisis is any event where the cost of a slow decision exceeds the cost of a wrong one. The generic moves are below; the per-type playbooks are what actually differ.
+ ## The Universal First Three
+ 1. **Name the clock.** What forces the deadline — cash, a regulator, a journalist's deadline, a customer's board meeting? Every crisis has one, and until you name it you will optimize the wrong thing.
+ 2. **Assign one decision-maker and one scribe.** Committees dissolve in crisis. The scribe timestamps every fact learned and every decision made; you will need that timeline for the board, counsel, or the post-mortem, and reconstructing it later is how people misremember themselves into trouble.
+ 3. **Separate facts from inferences in writing** before you communicate anything. Every crisis that got worse in public did so by asserting an inference that later turned out false.
+ Then set a cadence: internal sync every 4 hours on day one, daily by day three. A crisis without a cadence becomes a permanent state.
+ ## Cash Crisis
+ 1. Freeze: no new hires, no new vendors, no discretionary spend, all POs through you. Same day, before the plan exists.
+ 2. Get the real number: cash in bank, committed payables, payroll dates for the next 90 days. Collections chased personally — a receivable is cash that already exists.
+ 3. Compute the bear case (revenue flat, nothing new closes) and size the cut to hold months-to-milestone + 6 (SKILL.md rule 5). Cut once (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: layoffs).
+ 4. Call the two investors most likely to bridge before you call anyone else — insider willingness is the market signal, and if they decline you have your answer about the priced round too (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: fundraising).
+ 5. Payroll and payroll taxes are not a lever. Missing payroll converts a business problem into a personal-liability problem for officers and ends the company's ability to hire again (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: shutdown).
+ 6. Tell the board before the cut is designed, not after — a board that learns of a cash cliff at the meeting stops advising and starts governing.
+ ## Security Breach / Data Incident
+ 1. Contain before you diagnose: rotate credentials, revoke sessions and tokens, isolate the affected system. Preserve logs and images first — containment that destroys evidence costs you the investigation.
+ 2. Engage counsel early, and route the technical investigation through counsel where privilege is available; get the forensics firm in the same call.
+ 3. Notification clocks are legal, not editorial: the EU GDPR requires notifying the supervisory authority within 72 hours of becoming aware (Art. 33); US state breach laws, sector rules, and enterprise contracts each impose their own, and customer contracts are often stricter than statute. Pull the actual clauses from your top contracts on day one.
+ 4. Communicate what is confirmed, when it was confirmed, what customers should do, and when the next update lands. Never estimate the record count out loud before forensics does.
+ 5. The follow-through is the reputation event: publish the root cause and the systemic fix. Silence after the incident reads as concealment (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: communication).
+ ## Executive Misconduct or Fraud
+ 1. Do not investigate it yourself. Notify the board chair or audit committee, retain independent counsel, preserve documents and issue a litigation hold.
+ 2. Suspend access — systems, banking, signing authority — before the conversation, not after.
+ 3. Assume the person will contact employees, customers, or investors. Decide the internal message before the rumor does.
+ 4. Where money moved: separate the personnel question from the recovery question and from the disclosure question. They have different deadlines and different audiences (investors, insurer, bank, auditor).
+ 5. Do not settle quietly to avoid embarrassment when the conduct affected employees; a suppressed pattern surfaces later at ten times the cost.
+ ## Outage / Product Failure
+ 1. Status page inside 30 minutes, updated on a fixed interval even when there is nothing new — the interval is the message.
+ 2. One voice externally, one incident commander internally, engineering left alone to fix it.
+ 3. Customer-impact list first, root cause second. Your top accounts hear from a human, not from the status page.
+ 4. Credits and SLA: apply them proactively before customers ask; the ones who ask were already leaving.
+ 5. Post-mortem published to affected customers within a week: timeline, cause, what changes. Blameless internally, specific externally.
+ ## Key Person Leaves or Is Incapacitated
+ 1. Name an interim owner the same day, in writing, with decision authority — ambiguity here is what causes second and third departures.
+ 2. Knowledge transfer while you have goodwill: credentials, in-flight commitments, relationships, the undocumented things.
+ 3. Tell the team before the market does, and tell them who to go to now. Tell customers who own the relationship yourself.
+ 4. Then decide interim-versus-search deliberately (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: people); a rushed exec hire in a shaken org is the classic double failure.
+ 5. Prevention has a name: no single person holds the only copy of a credential, a customer relationship, or a critical system's knowledge. Audit the bus factor quarterly.
+ ## Press / Public Attack
+ 1. Verify the claim before you respond to it. Half of crises are a true fact framed badly, which is a very different response from a false claim.
+ 2. One spokesperson, one written line, no improvisation. Every employee gets the line and the instruction to forward inquiries.
+ 3. Correct facts, do not fight framing. Arguing framing extends the story by a news cycle; new facts end it.
+ 4. Respond where the audience is, not where the attack is; a thread does not require a press release.
+ 5. Employees and customers hear from you before or at the same time as the public — never after (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: communication).
+ ## Losing the Largest Customer
+ 1. Get the real reason within 48 hours from the economic buyer, not from your champion, and not from the account manager who wants it to be price.
+ 2. Save attempt or dignified exit — decide fast. A save that costs a roadmap quarter and a 40% discount is often a loss with extra steps (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: customers).
+ 3. Re-run the plan without them: revenue, runway, the covenant or milestone they were carrying. This is a board notification, immediately.
+ 4. Then fix the concentration, not just the account.
+ ## Regulatory Inquiry or Legal Demand
+ 1. Litigation hold first: suspend deletion policies company-wide, in writing, the day you learn of it.
+ 2. Single point of contact; no employee responds directly. Instruct the team not to speculate in writing — internal messages are discoverable, and a joke becomes evidence.
+ 3. Distinguish inquiry from action; do not volunteer scope. Answer what is asked, completely and accurately.
+ 4. Notify insurers (D&O, cyber) within the policy's notice window — late notice voids coverage more often than the claim itself fails (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance).
+ ## Crisis Principles
+ - The team mirrors your demeanor — move fast, never visibly panic; do the panicking with your confidants (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: ceo-operating-system).
+ - Communicate more than feels necessary; silence creates a rumor vacuum, and the rumor is always worse than the fact.
+ - Deal with the cash crisis first regardless of what else is burning: cash converts any other crisis into a fatal one.
+ - One decision-maker, stated by name, for the duration.
+ - Document everything contemporaneously; memory reorganizes itself in your favor within a week.
+ - Set the exit condition when you enter: what has to be true for this to stop being run as a crisis. Wartime mode with no declared end erodes the team faster than the crisis did (SKILL.md rule 8).

