+ # Customers — Where the CEO Belongs in the Account
+ The CEO is a scarce, high-signal resource. Spend it where a peer-level relationship changes the outcome, and nowhere else — a CEO in every deal teaches the buyer that no deal is real without one.
+ ## When The CEO Shows Up
+ | Situation | CEO's role | Not the CEO's role |
+ |---|---|---|
+ |---|---|---|
+ | Deal above `concentration_threshold` (default 10% of revenue) | Peer relationship with their exec sponsor, commitment on roadmap and support | Running the sales process |
+ | Strategic first customer in a new segment | Design partner, personally on the calls | Support escalation queue |
+ | Churn signal in a top-10 account | The call within 48 hours, asking the hard question | Discount authority handed out on the call |
+ | Escalation that has already failed twice | Own it, name the fix and the date, follow up personally | Debugging it live |
+ | Renewal of the reference customer | Ask for the renewal directly, and for the reference | Negotiating line items |
+ | Anything else | Stay out; back the account owner publicly | Bypassing your own team to look responsive |
+ Rule: when the CEO enters an account, the account owner stays in every conversation. A CEO who becomes the customer's contact has just removed their own team's authority, and the account never goes back.
+ ## Customer Contact Cadence
+ - Pre-PMF: five customer conversations a week, run by a founder, no exceptions and no delegation. This is the job, not research.
+ - Post-PMF: the CEO talks to at least one customer a week and one churned customer a month. The churned ones are the only honest source of what is actually broken.
+ - Concentration review quarterly: revenue by account, top-5 share, and which accounts renew in the next two quarters.
+ - Read raw support tickets or sales-call recordings monthly. Summaries arrive filtered by whoever is being evaluated on them (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: metrics).
+ ## Concentration Risk
+ One customer above `concentration_threshold` (default 10% of revenue) is a board topic; above 2× it — 20% at the default — it is the company's largest single risk and it belongs on the risk page of every board deck. Consequences worth naming before they arrive:
+ - Their procurement dictates your terms, your roadmap, and eventually your pricing.
+ - Acquirers and lenders discount valuation for it, sometimes with an explicit haircut, and diligence will find it (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: exit).
+ - Losing them is not a bad quarter; it is a restructuring event (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: crisis).
+ Mitigation is boring and slow: a named diversification target with a date, multi-year contracts with the big account, and multi-threaded relationships (at least three people, not just the champion who could leave next month).
+ ## Churn Saves
+ 1. Get the truth from the economic buyer within 48 hours. Champions soften and reps report price; the buyer reports the real reason, which is usually value never realized, a champion who left, or a reorganization.
+ 2. Ask directly: what would have had to be true for you to renew? The answer is either buyable or it is not, and you find out in one question.
+ 3. Decide save-or-release deliberately. A save that costs a roadmap quarter plus a 40% discount is a loss with extra steps, and worse, it is now the precedent in your renewal book.
+ 4. Offer a genuine fix with a date and an owner, not a discount. Discount-only saves churn on the next cycle at the lower price.
+ 5. Whatever happens, run the exit well: clean data export, no hostage tactics. Churned customers return more often than anyone expects, and they talk to your prospects.
+ ## Firing a Customer
+ Real and sometimes necessary. The candidates: support cost far above their revenue, roadmap distortion for a segment you are exiting, abuse of your staff, or contract terms you cannot repeat and cannot keep.
+ Do it with a long runway — a renewal not offered, 90+ days of notice, help migrating, and a written record. Never with a price increase designed to make them leave: they may accept it, and now you are locked in with a resentful customer at a number you have to defend to everyone else.
+ ## Advisory Boards and Design Partners
+ - A customer advisory board of 8-12 works when you bring real decisions to it; it dies when it becomes a product roadshow. Meet twice a year, share something not yet public, and act visibly on at least one thing they said.
+ - Design partners get early access and influence in exchange for engagement and a reference — write both sides down. A design partner who pays nothing and commits nothing is a friendly user.
+ - Never let a single design partner's requirements become the product. Build what three of them independently asked for; the rest is custom work you are not being paid for (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: pivot).
+ ## Traps
+ | Trap | Why it fails | Do instead |
+ | CEO becomes the escalation path | Every account learns to skip your team; the org stops owning outcomes | Enter with the owner, exit with the owner |
+ | Promising roadmap in a sales call | Engineering learns of the commitment from the customer | "I'll answer that by Friday" — then check with the owner |
+ | Reading only the wins | Post-mortem your losses and churns instead (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: competition) | Monthly loss and churn review, raw |
+ | Treating one loud account as the market | Roadmap bends toward whoever escalates hardest | Weight by cohort and revenue concentration |
+ | Letting a big deal rewrite the contract | One customer's terms become the floor for the next audit or acquirer | Legal red lines set in advance (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance) |

### decisions.md  [docs]
- 2. Everyone writes reasons **independently and silently** before any discussion — this step blocks anchoring on the senior voice and is the entire method; skip it and you get polite agreement.
+ 2. Everyone writes their reasons **independently, in writing, before anyone speaks** — this step blocks anchoring on the senior voice and is the entire method; skip it and you get polite agreement.
+ ## Deciding With Missing Information
+ The information you want does not exist for most consequential calls. Convert the fog into something decidable:
+ 1. Write the decision as a bet: "we believe X, so we are doing Y, and we expect Z by date D."
+ 2. Name the two or three assumptions the bet rests on, and rank them by how much you would pay to know the truth. Buy only the top one — a week of research, five customer calls, a small test.
+ 3. Set the falsifier before acting: the observation that means you were wrong (SKILL.md rule 9). "Wrong" needs to be observable, dated, and cheap to check.
+ 4. Price the option value of waiting explicitly against the cost of delay. Waiting is a decision with a cost, and treating it as neutral is how quarters disappear.
+ 5. Pick the reversible version of the bet where one exists — a pilot, one segment, one quarter — but only when it produces the same information. A "safe" pilot that cannot falsify the assumption is procrastination with a project plan.
+ ## Decision Rights by Class
+ Publish this once; it removes most escalation traffic.
+ | Class | Who decides | Consultation |
+ |---|---|---|
+ | One-way doors: pivot, layoff, exec exit, term sheet, sale, shutdown | CEO, with the board informed or approving | Pre-mortem, board pre-wire (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: board) |
+ | Cross-functional deadlock | CEO, only after both owners present a joint recommendation | Both owners in the room |
+ | Precedent-setting values calls | CEO | Exec team, then announced with the reasoning |
+ | Functional calls inside a strategy | The function owner, full stop | CEO on request, not by default |
+ | Spend above threshold, hiring above a level | CEO or the delegation matrix | Finance owner (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: finance) |
+ | Everything else | One named owner | Whoever they choose |
+ ## Razor Questions
+ Fast tests that resolve most stuck decisions:
+ - If we were starting today with no history here, would we choose this? (Grove's new-CEO test, applied to one decision)
+ - What would have to be true for the option I like least to be right?
+ - Is this expensive and reversible, or cheap and permanent? Only the second one deserves the anxiety.
+ - Who is the single person accountable for the outcome, and do they agree they are?
+ - Am I deciding, or waiting for someone to make it obvious? Waiting for obvious is a decision to be late.
+ - What does this cost if I am wrong, and can the company survive that? If yes, decide now.
+ - Would I defend this decision in front of the whole company, in these words?

### exit.md  [docs]
+ # Exit — When Someone Wants To Buy You
+ Companies are bought, not sold — but the ones that get bought well were ready, had a process, and had an alternative. The inbound email is the start of a negotiation whether you treat it as one or not.
+ ## The Inbound Approach
+ An unsolicited approach means one of three things: a real acquisition interest, a competitive intelligence exercise, or a junior corp-dev associate filling a pipeline quota. Distinguish before spending a week on it.
+ - Ask three questions on the first call: what triggered this now, who internally is sponsoring it, and what does their process and timeline look like? Vague answers to all three mean it is information gathering — share nothing beyond what is public.
+ - Never send the data room to establish credibility. Metrics you send to a competitor's corp-dev team do not come back.
+ - Do not name a price first. "What range would make this worth your board's time?" puts the number on their side of the table, where it belongs.
+ - Tell your board immediately, even for an approach you intend to decline. A board that learns later that you took M&A calls alone will never fully trust you again — and depending on your fiduciary duties, the meeting itself may need to be recorded (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance).
+ ## Deciding Whether To Run A Process
+ Sell when: the strategic buyer's price exceeds your standalone plan's risk-adjusted value; the category is consolidating and being third is fatal; the team is out of energy for another five years; or the capital required for the next stage is not available to you.
+ Do not sell because the quarter was bad, because fundraising is unpleasant, or because a single number sounds large before the waterfall (below).
+ Two structural realities to internalize before deciding:
+ - A single-bidder process is not a negotiation. Either create a real alternative (other bidders, or a credible plan to stay independent) or accept that the price is theirs to set.
+ - Your preference stack decides what "a good price" is. Run the waterfall before you form an opinion about a number.
+ ## The Waterfall — Run This First
+ Founders routinely misjudge an offer by an order of magnitude because they multiply their ownership percentage by the headline price.
+ Order of payment at close: transaction fees (bankers, legal) → debt → liquidation preferences in stack order (last money usually first) → participation, if the preferred participates → then common, pro rata → minus escrow and holdback amounts released later → minus the retention pool carved for employees, which usually comes off the top.
+ Worked example: 50M offer, 30M raised across rounds at 1x non-participating preference, 15% retention pool off the top, 10% escrow for 18 months. Sellers' pool at close: 50 − 7.5 (retention) = 42.5; preferred takes 30 unless converting yields more; common splits ~12.5, of which 10% sits in escrow. A founder with 20% of common sees roughly 2.25M at close, not 10M. Run this arithmetic before your first reaction to any number, and re-run it for each structure they propose (how the preferences got there → SKILL.md Quick Reference: fundraising).
+ ## Process Mechanics
+ | Phase | Duration | What decides the outcome |
+ |---|---|---|
+ | Preparation | 4-8 weeks | Clean cap table, IP assignment, contracts, financials that tie out (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance) |
+ | Outreach | 3-6 weeks | Multiple parties contacted in the same window — sequencing is what creates competition |
+ | Indications of interest | 2-4 weeks | Non-binding ranges; use them to shortlist, not to celebrate |
+ | Diligence and LOI | 4-8 weeks | Exclusivity starts; your leverage peaks the moment before you sign it |
+ | Confirmatory diligence and close | 4-12 weeks | Where price gets re-cut; surprises found here are always your fault in their telling |
+ Total 4-9 months, running in parallel with your day job. Assume the CEO loses 50% of their capacity for the duration, and staff accordingly — the classic disaster is a missed quarter during diligence, which hands the buyer a re-trade.
+ **Bankers**: worth it above roughly 25-50M in enterprise value, for competitive processes, or when the CEO cannot afford the bandwidth. Fees are a percentage of deal value with a minimum, plus expenses; negotiate the tail period (their claim on a deal after termination) and exclude the buyers you already know. Below that size, experienced M&A counsel plus your board network is usually the better trade.
+ ## Terms Beyond Price
+ - **Cash versus stock**: acquirer stock is a bet on their outcome with none of your control, and private-company stock may be unsellable for years. Ask about registration rights, lockups, and whether there is a liquidity path.
+ - **Escrow/holdback**: 10-15% for 12-18 months is convention; representation and warranty insurance can reduce or replace it, and is standard above certain deal sizes.
+ - **Earnouts**: assume they do not pay in full. Every earnout metric depends on decisions the buyer will control after close. If you accept one, tie it to a metric you can still influence and write in operating covenants — headcount, budget, and pricing authority.
+ - **Retention and employee outcomes**: what happens to unvested options, whether there is acceleration, and how the retention pool is allocated. Your team's outcome is a term you negotiate, not an afterthought — and single-trigger acceleration for everyone is a term the buyer will resist because it deletes their retention.
+ - **Founder commitments**: employment terms, vesting of the retention grant, non-compete scope and duration. Two years is a real cost; price it or narrow it.
+ - **Non-solicit and no-shop**: the no-shop period is your leverage handed over. Keep it as short as they will accept, with an outside date.
+ ## The Human Part
+ - Your team learns at signing, not before, but you decide the message before you sign (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: communication). Their first two questions are: do I have a job, and what happens to my equity. Have both answers ready.
+ - Key employees may be asked to sign new employment agreements as a closing condition. Find out who and tell them early enough that the deal does not hinge on a surprise conversation.
+ - Deals die. Roughly a third of signed LOIs do not close — run the company as if it will not close, because that is also your only leverage.
+ - The reference you leave with the acquirer's team and your own is what you take to the next company.

### finance.md  [docs]
- # Financial Scenarios and Modeling
- ## Runway Calculator
- Runway (months) = Cash Balance / Monthly Burn Rate
- ### Burn Rate Types
- | Type | Definition | Use Case |
- |------|------------|----------|
- | Gross burn | Total monthly expenses | Worst case planning |
- | Net burn | Expenses - Revenue | Normal planning |
- | Adjusted burn | Net burn with planned changes | Scenario modeling |
- ### Runway Zones
- | Runway | Status | Action |
- | 18+ months | Comfortable | Execute strategy |
- | 12-18 months | Start fundraising | Begin investor conversations |
- | 6-12 months | Urgent | Actively close round OR cut costs |
- | < 6 months | Crisis | Emergency measures, bridge round |
- ## Scenario Planning Framework
- Build three scenarios for any major decision:
- | Scenario | Assumptions | Outcome |
- |----------|-------------|---------|
- | **Base** | Current trajectory continues | Expected result |
- | **Bull** | Key metrics improve 20-30% | Upside potential |
- | **Bear** | Key metrics decline 20-30% | Downside risk |
- ### Variables to Model
- - Revenue growth rate
- - Churn/retention
- - CAC and payback period
- - Hiring pace
- - Unit economics
- - Market conditions
- ## Unit Economics Template
- | Metric | Formula | Target |
- |--------|---------|--------|
- | CAC | Total acquisition cost / New customers | < LTV/3 |
- | LTV | ARPU × Gross margin × Avg lifespan | > 3× CAC |
- | Payback | CAC / (ARPU × Gross margin) | < 12 months |
- | Gross margin | (Revenue - COGS) / Revenue | > 70% SaaS |
- | Magic number | Net new ARR / S&M spend | > 0.75 |
- ## Cash Flow Projections
- **Monthly model should include:**
- | Line | Description |
- |------|-------------|
- | Starting cash | Previous month ending |
- | Revenue | Collections, not bookings |
- | COGS | Direct costs of delivery |
- | Gross profit | Revenue - COGS |
- | OpEx | R&D, S&M, G&A |
- | Net burn | Gross profit - OpEx |
- | Ending cash | Starting - Net burn |
- ## Valuation Basics
- ### Revenue Multiples by Stage
- Rule-of-thumb ranges only — not published benchmarks. Multiples swing wildly with the funding cycle (2021 peaks were 2-3× these; 2023 troughs were roughly half), so pull live comps before anchoring on any number here.
- | Stage | Rule-of-thumb Multiple | Factors |
- |-------|------------------------|---------|
- | Seed | ~10-20× ARR | Team, TAM, vision |
- | Series A | ~8-15× ARR | Growth rate, retention |
- | Series B | ~6-12× ARR | Unit economics, moat |
- | Series C+ | ~5-10× ARR | Profitability path |
- *Multiples vary by market conditions, sector, and growth rate. Verify against current comparables — these are convention, not fact.*
- ### Valuation Drivers
- 1. **Growth rate** — Higher growth = higher multiple
- 2. **Retention** — Net dollar retention > 100% = expansion
- 3. **TAM** — Bigger market = bigger opportunity
- 4. **Gross margin** — SaaS > 70%, marketplace 40-60%
- 5. **Efficiency** — Rule of 40 (growth + margin > 40%)
- ## Budget Planning
- ### Zero-Based Budgeting
- Don't assume last year's budget is baseline. Justify every expense from zero.
- **Process:**
- 1. List all activities and their costs
- 2. Rank by impact on company goals
- 3. Allocate budget top-down until depleted
- 4. Cut everything below the line
- ### Headcount Planning
- | Role Type | Planning Horizon | Flexibility |
- |-----------|------------------|-------------|
- | Leadership | 12+ months | Low - hire slow |
- | Core team | 6-12 months | Medium |
- | Contractors | 0-3 months | High |
- ### Monthly Review
- - P&L vs budget variance
- - Cash position and runway
- - Key metrics vs plan
- - Expense approval queue
- ### Quarterly Review
- - Reforecast for remaining year
- - Adjust hiring plans
- - Board financial update
- - Scenario planning refresh
- ## Crisis Financial Playbook
- ### When Cash is Tight
- | Lever | Speed | Impact |
- |-------|-------|--------|
- | Freeze hiring | Immediate | Low |
- | Reduce marketing | 30 days | Medium |
- | Renegotiate contracts | 60 days | Medium |
- | Layoffs | 30 days | High |
- | Pivot/shutdown products | 90 days | High |
- ### Bridge Round Considerations
- - Term should match time to milestone
- - Existing investors first
- - Valuation: often flat or down
- - Don't bridge to nowhere - have a plan
+ # Finance — The CEO's Version
+ Enough finance to allocate capital, defend a plan, and never be surprised by your own bank balance. Detailed modeling, the monthly close, forecast mechanics, and dilution math belong to `cfo`; what follows is what the CEO must be able to compute in their head and defend in a board meeting.
+ Contents: [Runway and the Zones](#runway-and-the-zones) · [Default Alive](#default-alive) · [Capital Allocation](#capital-allocation--the-actual-job) · [Unit Economics](#unit-economics) · [Scenarios](#scenarios) · [Valuation, Honestly](#valuation-honestly) · [Emergency Levers](#emergency-levers) · [Financial Governance](#financial-governance)
+ ## Runway and the Zones
+ Runway (months) = Cash balance / Net monthly burn
+ Net burn        = Cash operating expenses − cash collected (not revenue booked)
+ Collections, not bookings. A plan built on invoiced revenue with 60-day payment terms is a plan that runs out of cash two months before the spreadsheet says so.
+ This table is the single canonical home for the runway bands. Bands do not overlap; nothing else in the skill re-labels them. `runway_alarm_months` (default 12) is the top of the Start The Raise band — the month count at which cash becomes priority one.
+ | Runway (months) | Zone | Action |
+ | ≥18 | Comfortable | Execute the plan; opportunistic raise only if the market is hot |
+ | 12-17 | Start the raise (`runway_alarm_months`, default 12) | Cash becomes priority one: begin investor conversations — a round runs 3-6 months end to end, close included (SKILL.md rule 4) |
+ | 9-11 | Parallel paths | Raise and cut planning run at once; decide the cut trigger date now. 9 months is the hard floor to *start* a round |
+ | 6-8 | Urgent | Close a round, or cut to milestone + 6 months of runway (SKILL.md rule 5); below 9 you are negotiating from weakness |
+ | <6 | Crisis | Emergency levers, bridge, or wind-down decision while cash remains (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: crisis, shutdown) |
+ Three burn numbers, all useful: **gross burn** (total cash out — the worst case if revenue stops), **net burn** (gross minus collections — the planning number), and **adjusted burn** (net burn with committed changes applied — the number to use for any decision about the future).
+ ## Default Alive
+ Graham's test: at your current growth rate and burn, does revenue cross expenses before cash hits zero? Compute it with today's growth rate, not the plan's. Two consequences:
+ - Default dead means every plan is a fundraising plan whether or not it says so; put that in the board materials rather than presenting a plan that quietly depends on capital nobody has committed.
+ - Default alive is an option, not a destination: it means you can choose to raise rather than needing to, which is the only condition under which terms are good (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: fundraising).
+ ## Capital Allocation — The Actual Job
+ The CEO's finance job is deciding where the next marginal dollar goes. Compare every candidate on the same three axes: payback period in months, what it does to the strategic bet, and reversibility if it fails.
+ - Fund things whose payback fits inside your runway. A 24-month payback with 14 months of cash is a bet on the next round, not an investment.
+ - Sales and marketing spend is only scalable when CAC payback holds at scale — the first cohort's efficiency does not survive a 3x budget increase, so step it up in increments and re-measure.
+ - Headcount is the least reversible allocation you make: it is a multi-year commitment with a severance tail (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: layoffs). Contractors and agencies cost more per unit and are worth it while the need is unproven.
+ - Say no in a way that survives: "not this quarter, and here is what would have to be true" beats an unexplained rejection that comes back monthly.
+ ## Unit Economics
+ | Metric | Formula | Convention |
+ |--------|---------|-----------|
+ | CAC | Fully loaded sales + marketing spend / new customers, same period | Include salaries and tooling, not just ad spend |
+ | LTV | ARPA × gross margin % × average lifetime (1 / churn rate) | Break it out by segment or it means nothing |
+ | LTV:CAC | LTV / CAC | 3:1 is the usual health line; well above it usually means underinvestment, not excellence |
+ | CAC payback | CAC / (monthly ARPA × gross margin %) | Under 12 months for SMB, under 18-24 tolerated in enterprise |
+ | Gross margin | (Revenue − COGS) / Revenue | 70%+ for software; below that, growth costs cash |
+ | Burn multiple | Net burn / net new ARR (Sacks) | Under 1 is excellent, 1-2 is workable, above 2 means growth is being bought |
+ | Rule of 40 | Growth rate % + profit margin % | A late-stage screen; misleading below Series B |
+ Worked payback example: CAC 12,000; ARPA 1,000/month; gross margin 75% → 12,000 / 750 = 16 months. With 14 months of runway, that channel cannot be scaled with your own cash — it is a use of the next round, and should be presented that way.
+ ## Scenarios
+ Build three, always with the same structure, and define the bear case before you need it:
+ | Scenario | Assumptions | Used for |
+ |----------|-------------|----------|
+ | Base | Current trajectory, closed pipeline only | The plan you commit to the board |
+ | Bull | Key metrics improve 20-30% | Where you would deploy upside, decided in advance |
+ | Bear | Revenue flat, nothing unsigned closes, one large customer leaves | Cut sizing (SKILL.md rule 5) and covenant headroom |
+ The bear case is not "20% worse". It is the specific set of things that plausibly go wrong together, because they correlate: a bad quarter, a churned top account, and a closed funding market arrive in the same year for the same reason.
+ Every scenario names its **trigger and its action**: "if Q2 bookings are under X by June 30, we execute the cut on July 15." Scenarios without triggers are documents; scenarios with triggers are decisions already made calmly (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: decisions).
+ ## Valuation, Honestly
+ Revenue multiples are convention that swings with the funding cycle — the same company was worth two to three times more at the 2021 peak than in the 2023 trough. Use these as a sanity check only, and pull live comparables before anchoring anything on them.
+ | Stage | Rule-of-thumb ARR multiple | What moves it |
+ |-------|---------------------------|---------------|
+ | Seed | ~10-20× | Team, market, narrative — largely not the multiple, it is priced on ownership targets |
+ | Series A | ~8-15× | Growth rate and retention |
+ | Series B | ~6-12× | Unit economics and evidence of a moat |
+ | Series C+ | ~5-10× | Efficiency and a path to profit |
+ Drivers in order of weight: growth rate, net revenue retention, gross margin, market size, and efficiency. Note the asymmetry founders miss — valuation is set by your ownership dilution target as often as by your metrics: an investor needing 20% at a 5M check produces a 25M post-money regardless of your model.
+ ## Emergency Levers
+ | Lever | Cash effect visible | Cost |
+ |-------|---------------------|------|
+ | Freeze hiring and open reqs | Immediate | None; open reqs are committed cash nobody counts |
+ | Cut discretionary and vendor spend, renegotiate tooling | 1-30 days | Low; annual contracts are often renegotiable mid-term under threat of non-renewal |
+ | Collections push and payment terms | 15-45 days | Free money; a receivable is cash you already earned |
+ | Marketing and contractor reductions | 30 days | Pipeline in 1-2 quarters |
+ | Founder and exec comp deferral | 30 days | Symbolically large, financially small; use it for signal, not for runway |
+ | Layoffs | 30-60 days, net of severance | Highest impact, one-way door (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: layoffs) |
+ | Sale-leaseback, venture debt, factoring | 30-90 days | Adds covenants and priority ahead of equity |
+ | Killing a product line | 90 days | Frees engineering and support; usually overdue |
+ Order them by cash-per-unit-of-damage, and pull the cheap ones before you touch people. But do not spend three months on small levers to avoid a cut that arithmetic already requires — that is how companies end up cutting twice.
+ - Weekly: cash balance and collections. Monthly: closed financials, plan variance, updated runway. Quarterly: reforecast the rest of the year, revisit hiring, refresh scenarios (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: metrics).
+ - A variance over 10% on any major line gets an explanation and an owner, not a note.
+ - Two people on every payment above a threshold, and the CEO does not approve their own expenses. Dual control is the cheapest fraud prevention that exists (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance).
+ - Know your bank concentration and your insured limits. Cash sitting in one uninsured account at one institution is a risk nobody thinks about until the week it matters.

### first-90-days.md  [docs]
+ # The First 90 Days in the Seat
+ Applies to an externally hired CEO, an internally promoted one, and a founder formally taking the role for the first time. The mistake shared by all three is acting on the pattern that made them successful before diagnosing which situation they are actually in.
+ ## Diagnose the Situation First (Watkins, STARS)
+ | Situation | What it needs | The fatal mismatch |
+ |---|---|---|
+ |---|---|---|
+ | **Start-up** | Build structure from nothing, hire, choose focus | Running a listening tour when there is nothing to listen to |
+ | **Turnaround** | Fast, visible, unpopular cuts; centralize | Consensus-building while cash burns (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: crisis) |
+ | **Accelerated growth** | Scaling systems, hiring ahead, process (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: scaling) | Adding controls that slow the thing that is working |
+ | **Realignment** | Convince a successful org that it is heading for trouble | Declaring an emergency nobody believes yet |
+ | **Sustaining success** | Protect what works, find the next S-curve | Changing things to demonstrate you are in charge |
+ Most companies are a mix — name the dominant one per function; the go-to-market org may be a turnaround while engineering is sustaining success. Your first-90-days plan differs per function accordingly.
+ ## Days 1-30: Learn, Deliberately
+ - **Listening tour with a fixed question set** so answers are comparable: what should we keep, what should we change, what would you do in my seat, what are we not talking about? Same four questions to 20-40 people across levels and functions.
+ - Talk to 10 customers and 5 churned ones in the first month. In week one for a turnaround.
+ - Read before you ask: the last four board decks, last year's plan versus actuals, the org chart with tenure, the top 20 accounts, the last two exec departures' reasons.
+ - Meet every board member 1:1 and ask each what success looks like at 12 months and what they would have done differently. Divergent answers between directors are the most important thing you will learn this month (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: board).
+ - Establish the numbers you will run on and their definitions before you inherit someone else's dashboard (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: metrics).
+ - Say almost nothing definitive in public. Every early opinion becomes policy whether you meant it or not.
+ Exception: if the company is out of cash or in an active crisis, skip to acting on day one and run the listening tour in parallel. Runway under `runway_alarm_months` overrides the calendar (SKILL.md rule 4).
+ ## Days 31-60: Decide
+ - Publish your read: what you learned, what stays, what changes, what you are still deciding and when you will decide it. A written memo, not a slide deck (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: communication).
+ - Name the three priorities and the kill list. The kills are what make the priorities credible (SKILL.md rule 2).
+ - Establish the operating cadence — weekly exec, monthly all-hands, board rhythm — and hold it exactly. Predictability is the first thing the org is testing.
+ - Take on the one visible problem everyone has complained about and fix it. An early win is not a symbolic gesture; it is evidence that raising a problem produces action.
+ - Do not reorg yet unless the situation is a turnaround. A reorg in the first 60 days uses your only credibility on a change you cannot yet justify.
+ ## Days 61-90: Commit
+ - Publish the plan for the next two quarters with owners, dates, and the metrics that will show progress.
+ - Complete the exec team read. By day 90 you should know who stays, who needs a different role, and who leaves. Working rule, not a survey finding: the exec you privately doubt in month two is the one you will wish you had moved in month six — act on the doubt rather than waiting for evidence that convinces everyone else (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: people).
+ - Move on the clearest exec case first, well and generously. The org is watching how you treat someone's exit more than who you exited.
+ - Rebuild the bench you will need for the plan; do not backfill the org you inherited.
+ - Formal 90-day review with the board: here is what I found, here is the plan, here is what I need from you.
+ ## If You Were Promoted From Within
+ - Your peers are now your reports, and the relationship has changed whether you address it or not. Address it explicitly in the first week, one by one.
+ - The person who wanted the job and did not get it: talk to them in the first 48 hours, be direct about their path, and accept that they may leave. Pretending it did not happen guarantees it goes underground.
+ - Hand over your old job completely and visibly. Keeping a piece of it tells everyone the new role has not started.
+ - Your advantage is context; your risk is that you inherit the previous strategy's assumptions unexamined. Run the listening tour anyway — people will tell the new CEO things they never told the peer.
+ ## If You Are a Founder Taking the Title Formally
+ - The change is real even if the work looks the same: you now have to be legible to a board, a team, and eventually a buyer. Legibility means written strategy, kept cadence, and decisions with owners and dates (SKILL.md rule 9).
+ - What you should keep doing personally: strategy, top hires, the surface where your judgment is the product. What must be handed over: the queue of approvals you have been absorbing (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: scaling).
+ ## Traps
+ | Trap | Why it fails | Do instead |
+ | Big changes before diagnosis | You break the thing that was working and nobody tells you which one it was | 30 days of structured listening (crisis excepted) |
+ | Importing your old playbook wholesale | It was fitted to a different situation and stage | Diagnose STARS per function, then adapt |
+ | Bringing three former colleagues in month one | The org reads it as a takeover and stops telling you things | One trusted hire early at most; earn the rest |
+ | Trashing your predecessor | Signals insecurity and insults everyone who worked for them | Neutral about the past, specific about the future |
+ | Promising an answer date you then miss | Your first broken commitment sets the standard | Commit to fewer dates and hit all of them |
+ | Skipping the board 1:1s | You discover the board is not aligned during your first hard decision | Individual 1:1s in the first two weeks (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: board) |

