- **Total: 3-6 months.** The single most expensive mistake is starting late, because it converts every other decision into a desperation decision.
+ Contents: [When to Raise](#when-to-raise) · [Dilution Base Rates](#dilution-base-rates) · [Timeline](#timeline) · [Leverage](#leverage-is-manufactured-not-found) · [Term Sheet](#term-sheet-what-each-term-actually-costs) · [Closing](#closing-where-deals-die) · [Bridge Rounds](#bridge-rounds) · [SAFEs and Notes](#safes-and-notes-the-conversion-surprise) · [Down Rounds and Recaps](#down-rounds-and-recaps) · [Venture Debt](#venture-debt-and-non-dilutive-capital) · [Secondaries](#secondaries) · [When the Round Does Not Come Together](#when-the-round-does-not-come-together)
+ | Runway at `runway_alarm_months` (default 12) | Start conversations — a round runs 3-6 months end to end, close included, so 9 months is the hard floor to start (SKILL.md rule 4; canonical zone table → SKILL.md Quick Reference: finance) |
+ **Total: 3-6 months, close included** — the three phases above sum to 14-26 weeks; do not add the close again on top. The single most expensive mistake is starting late, because it converts every other decision into a desperation decision.
+ ## SAFEs and Notes: The Conversion Surprise
+ Stacked pre-priced instruments are the most common source of a founder discovering they own less than they thought at the Series A.
+ - Every SAFE and note converts at the priced round, usually at the better of its cap or the round price. Several caps from different years convert at different prices simultaneously.
+ - **Post-money SAFEs (the current standard) fix the investor's percentage, so all dilution from later SAFEs falls on you, not on them.** Two 3M post-money SAFEs are not "6M of dilution split" — each holder's percentage is locked, and the founders absorb the difference.
+ - Maintain a running pro-forma of the cap table as-converted, including the new option pool, before you agree a price. The number that matters is founder ownership after conversion and after the pool, not the headline valuation (`cfo` for the model; the ledger → SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance).
+ ## Down Rounds and Recaps
+ - A clean down round is survivable and common in a repricing market. Structure — participating preferred, multiple liquidation preferences, full ratchet — is what does lasting damage, because it sits on the cap table through every future round and exit (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: exit).
+ - Anti-dilution adjusts existing investors' conversion price automatically; broad-based weighted average is the standard, and full ratchet reprices their whole stake to the new low. Know which you signed before you price a down round.
+ - A recapitalization (converting preferred to common, restarting the stack) is the tool when the preference overhang exceeds any plausible exit value and the team has no reason to stay. It is a board and stockholder process with real conflicts — independent process matters (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance).
+ - Refresh the team's equity as part of any down round or recap. Employees holding underwater options with a repriced cap table above them have no economic reason to stay, and they will do that arithmetic.
+ ## Venture Debt and Non-Dilutive Capital
+ - Venture debt extends runway without dilution but adds covenants, warrants, and a claim ahead of equity (payment order → SKILL.md Quick Reference: shutdown). The usable window is when you are healthy: lenders size against your last round and pull availability precisely when you need it.
+ - Read the material-adverse-change clause and any minimum-cash covenant. Those are the terms that convert a bad quarter into a demand for repayment.
+ - Revenue-based financing and receivables factoring are expensive per dollar and fast; they fit a bounded, provable need (a hardware build, a collections gap), not general runway.
+ - Never take debt to avoid an equity conversation you will still have to have in six months, at a lower valuation, with a lender ahead of you.
+ ## Secondaries
+ Founder secondaries — selling some of your own shares in a round — are normal at Series B and later, typically a modest slice of the round, and require the lead's and often the board's agreement. Two things to know: taking too much reads as reduced conviction and can kill the round, and any secondary establishes a share price with tax and 409A implications (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance).
+ ## When the Round Does Not Come Together
+ Diagnose honestly instead of adding meetings: consistent feedback across investors is the signal (metrics, market, or team), while inconsistent feedback usually means the story is unclear. Then act on the timeline you have — cut to reach default alive rather than raising desperately (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: finance, layoffs), go back to the insiders with a specific milestone-based bridge, or accept a smaller round from the fund that actually believes. A round abandoned cleanly and reopened in six months with new evidence beats a round that limps for nine and closes on structure.

### governance.md  [docs]
+ # Governance and Corporate Hygiene
+ Boring until it is fatal. Every item below is cheap to maintain and expensive to fix retroactively — and they get discovered at the worst possible moment: during diligence for a round or a sale, when you have no leverage and a clock.
+ ## Fiduciary Duties in Practice
+ As a director you owe duty of care (be informed, deliberate properly) and duty of loyalty (the company's interest over your own). What that means operationally:
+ - Conflicts get disclosed and the conflicted party leaves the vote. This includes you: your own compensation, a company you also invest in, a contract with a relative.
+ - Decisions on major matters need a documented record — materials circulated in advance, deliberation, alternatives considered. The record is the defense; its absence is the exposure.
+ - The board's duty runs to the corporation and its stockholders, not to the investor who appointed a director. An investor-director advocating solely for their fund's outcome is a governance issue you should name.
+ - Once a sale of control becomes inevitable, the duty shifts toward maximizing value for stockholders (the Delaware Revlon line of cases) — process matters more here than in any other decision (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: exit).
+ - On insolvency, creditors gain standing to bring derivative claims. Decisions made near the line get scrutinized after the fact; document reasoning contemporaneously (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: shutdown).
+ ## The Records That Must Exist
+ | Record | Cadence | Consequence of neglect |
+ |---|---|---|
+ | Board minutes | Every meeting, drafted within 2 weeks, approved at the next | No evidence of process; option grants of uncertain validity |
+ | Written consents for grants, hires, financings | Per action | Options granted without board approval are voidable — a routine diligence finding |
+ | Signed stock and option ledger, single source of truth | Continuous | Cap table disputes at the worst moment (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: exit) |
+ | IP assignment from every founder, employee, contractor | At start of engagement, no exceptions | The most common small-deal blocker: a contractor owns part of your product |
+ | Executed employment and contractor agreements | At start | Misclassification exposure, unclear notice terms |
+ | Corporate filings, franchise tax, registered agent | Annually | Administrative dissolution — which does happen, quietly |
+ | Insurance: D&O, E&O, cyber, employment practices | Annual review | D&O before your first outside director; late notice on any policy voids coverage (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: crisis) |
+ ## Equity Administration
+ - **409A valuation** (US): refresh at least every 12 months and on any material event — a new priced round, an acquisition offer, a major change in results. Grants priced off a stale 409A create tax exposure for your employees, which is your problem, not theirs.
+ - **Board approval before communication.** The grant date is the approval date; promising a number in an offer letter and approving it three months later is a repricing problem.
+ - **83(b) elections**: 30 days from grant, no extensions, and this catches founders and early employees with restricted stock (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: cofounders).
+ - **Option exercise window**: the standard 90 days post-termination means a departing employee must find cash and pay tax to keep what they earned. Extending it (to 5-10 years) is a real, one-time governance decision with real tax consequences for the employee (ISO status converts) — decide it deliberately and apply it consistently.
+ - **The pool**: sized against an 18-month hiring plan; a pool created pre-money dilutes only existing holders (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: fundraising).
+ - **QSBS eligibility** (US, §1202) depends on entity type, gross assets at issuance, and holding period, and the rules were amended in 2025. Before any restructuring, reincorporation, or redemption, ask counsel what it does to §1202 — the mistake is unrecoverable.
+ ## Employment and Compliance
+ - **Contractor versus employee** is decided by how the work happens, not by the contract's label. Full-time, directed, integrated workers who were paid on invoices generate back taxes, penalties, and benefits claims.
+ - **Multi-state and multi-country employment**: one remote hire creates payroll registration, tax nexus, and sometimes sales-tax obligations in that jurisdiction. Know where you have employees before finance discovers it (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: international).
+ - **Compliance regimes** worth planning a quarter ahead of the deal that requires them: SOC 2 Type II requires an observation window (commonly 3-12 months) — you cannot start it the week enterprise procurement asks; GDPR and similar regimes need a lawful basis, DPAs with subprocessors, and a breach process (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: crisis); HIPAA, PCI, and sector rules gate entire market segments.
+ - **Data retention and deletion policy**: adopt one and follow it. Selective deletion after a dispute begins is spoliation; a consistently applied policy is a defense.
+ ## Board Operations
+ - Committees when the board reaches ~5 members or a raise requires them: audit (financial oversight, auditor relationship) and compensation (exec pay, equity grants — and the CEO does not sit on their own comp decision).
+ - Independent directors: recruit them yourself, deliberately, before you need the tiebreaking vote. Who selects the independent is the negotiation (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: board).
+ - Meeting materials circulated 5-7 days in advance, the same lead time as the board packet (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: board); decisions requiring a vote flagged as such in the agenda so nobody claims surprise.
+ - Written consent for routine approvals between meetings keeps grants and hires from waiting a quarter — but consents still need to be signed, filed, and reflected in the ledger.
+ - Observers are not directors: they have no vote and no duty, and information rights should be written, not assumed.
+ ## The Diligence-Readiness Drill
+ Once a year, spend a day assembling what a buyer or lead investor would ask for: cap table with every instrument, all board minutes and consents, IP assignments for everyone who ever wrote code, executed customer and vendor contracts with change-of-control clauses flagged, financials that tie to the bank, tax filings, insurance, and a list of every jurisdiction you have employees or customers in. Whatever is missing takes weeks to reconstruct — find out on a calm day, not during a signed LOI with an outside date (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: exit).

### international.md  [docs]
+ # International — The First Market Outside Home
+ Expansion fails on the same thing almost every time: it was a growth initiative when it should have been a decision about where the company's next scarce quarter of attention goes.
+ ## Should You Go At All
+ Prerequisites, all four, before the first hire abroad:
+ 1. **A repeatable motion at home.** Expansion multiplies your go-to-market; multiplying something unrepeatable produces a second unrepeatable thing in a harder timezone.
+ 2. **Pull, not push**: inbound demand, unprompted signups, or customers already using you from that market. Entering without existing pull means paying for demand generation and localization at once.
+ 3. **Runway for the ramp**: expect 12-18 months to first meaningful revenue in a new country, longer for enterprise sales cycles. Budget accordingly and never fund it from the number your home market is expected to hit.
+ 4. **An owner who can be sent.** The single largest determinant of country success is the first leader, and no process compensates for hiring the wrong one.
+ Choose the market on: existing pull, language and contract-law proximity, buyer similarity, and regulatory cost — in that order. Market size ranks below all four, because the biggest market is where your competitors already are and where your playbook is least likely to transfer.
+ ## Employment: Entity or EOR
+ | Path | Fits | Cost and friction |
+ |---|---|---|
+ | Contractor | One person, exploratory, short | Misclassification risk is highest here and grows with duration and exclusivity (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance) |
+ | Employer of record | 1-10 people, testing a market, speed | A monthly per-employee fee; live in weeks; some countries and equity arrangements are unsupported |
+ | Own entity | 10+ people, permanent commitment, local invoicing needed | Months to establish, ongoing accounting, payroll, and statutory filings in-country; hardest to unwind |
+ Default: EOR for the first hires, entity when headcount or invoicing requirements force it. Reversal is the expensive direction — an entity you no longer need takes months and real money to dissolve.
+ **Permanent establishment** is the trap that surprises CEOs: a person in-country who negotiates or concludes contracts can create a taxable presence for the whole company, regardless of how you classify them. Ask a tax advisor before the first salesperson starts, not at the first audit.
+ ## What Localization Actually Means
+ Translation is the cheapest part and the least differentiating.
+ - **Pricing and currency**: local currency pricing, local payment methods (bank transfer and direct debit dominate in much of Europe; cards do not), and prices set to local willingness to pay rather than converted from home (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: pricing).
+ - **Tax and invoicing**: VAT/GST registration and thresholds, invoice format requirements, e-invoicing mandates in a growing number of countries. Getting this wrong blocks enterprise procurement from paying you.
+ - **Contracts**: governing law and jurisdiction, statutory consumer rights, mandatory local terms. Your standard MSA will be rejected by large buyers in many markets.
+ - **Data**: residency and transfer requirements — where the data lives is a purchase criterion in European and public-sector deals (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance).
+ - **Support hours**: a European enterprise customer with support that starts at 4pm local time will churn regardless of product quality.
+ - **Buying process**: procurement norms, security questionnaires, and required certifications differ; SOC 2 may not substitute for the local expectation.
+ ## The Country Lead
+ - Hire a local operator with a network in your buyer's world, not an expatriate manager from headquarters who will spend a year learning what a local knew on day one.
+ - Give them real authority — pricing latitude, hiring, and roadmap input — with a clear scope. An in-country leader who must escalate every decision to a timezone eight hours away loses to any local competitor.
+ - Their first 12-month goal is proving the motion in-market with a handful of reference customers, not a revenue target copied from home and divided by two.
+ - Do not send your second-best home-market seller as a promotion. Country launches fail on judgment, not effort.
+ ## Running a Multi-Timezone Company
+ - Define the overlap window and protect it. Two to four hours of shared working time is the minimum for a team that must decide together; below that, split ownership of whole domains rather than splitting one domain across the gap (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: scaling).
+ - Written by default. Anything decided verbally in one timezone is invisible in the other, and the remote office learns that decisions happen without them.
+ - Rotate meeting inconvenience. A recurring call that is always painful for the same office tells that office what it is worth.
+ - Deliberately transmit culture: the new country will develop its own operating norms within months. Visit in person early, and bring one of their people to headquarters (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: communication).
+ - Comp bands per market, published internally with the logic. Cross-country pay comparisons happen within weeks regardless of policy; the only choice is whether you explained it first (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: people).
+ ## Kill Criteria
+ Set them before launch: the metric, the threshold, and the date at which you either double down or exit. Exiting a country is a real project — employment termination law abroad is not at-will, entities take months to close, and customers need a transition plan. Budget the exit before you fund the entry.

### layoffs.md  [docs]
+ # Layoffs — Sizing, Selection, Notice, and the Day
+ The decision is arithmetic; the execution is choreography. Both get done badly by CEOs who have never run one, and the cost of doing it twice is measured in the people who stay.
+ ## Sizing (do this before anything else)
+ Target runway after the cut = months to the next fundable milestone + 6, computed on the bear case: revenue flat, no new round, no unsigned deal counted (SKILL.md rule 5).
+ Worked example: 900k cash, 150k/month net burn (6 months), milestone 9 months out. Required runway 15 months → required burn ≤ 60k/month → 90k/month must come out. Payroll is typically 60-80% of a startup's burn, so non-payroll levers alone rarely reach it: pull vendor, cloud, marketing, and contractor spend first, then size headcount against the remainder. Assume severance and unused-PTO payouts consume 1-2 months of the savings up front; the cut must be big enough to survive its own cost.
+ Three sizes exist: too small (you will be back within 9 months, and everyone knows it), right (the plan closes with margin), and too deep (you cannot execute the plan you just kept). Test for too deep: name the person who now does each critical function. If a name repeats three times, you cut into the plan.
+ ## Selection
+ Select by role and plan, never by tenure default, and never by manager popularity. Sequence:
+ 1. Redesign the org for the post-cut plan on a blank page: which functions exist, at what size, to hit which milestone.
+ 2. Map current people to that org. The gap is the cut. Doing it in this order is what makes the list defensible.
+ 3. Apply consistent criteria in writing: criticality of role to the plan, performance record, skill coverage. Document the criteria before names.
+ 4. Have counsel review the list for disparate impact by age, gender, race, disability, leave status, and recent protected activity. A list that unintentionally clusters on a protected class is a lawsuit even when every individual decision was sound.
+ 5. Keep the people whose absence would break the plan, not the people you enjoy most. Then check the inverse: an underperformer you were going to manage out belongs on this list, but a cut is not a substitute for a performance conversation you owed someone.
+ Cut managers proportionally. A layoff that only removes ICs leaves a supervision layer with nobody to supervise, and the survivors count.
+ ## Legal Clocks (verify against current counsel; these are the usual traps)
+ | Regime | Trigger | Requirement |
+ |---|---|---|
+ |---|---|---|
+ | US federal WARN | Employer with 100+ employees; 50+ affected at a single site, or a plant closing | 60 days' advance written notice, or pay in lieu |
+ | US state mini-WARN | Lower thresholds than federal (California starts at 75 employees; New York and New Jersey have their own) | Notice periods and definitions differ by state — check every state you employ in |
+ | Older-worker waivers (US, OWBPA) | Asking employees 40+ to release age claims | 21 days to consider (45 in a group program), 7 days to revoke, plus a disclosure of ages and job titles in the decisional unit |
+ | UK collective consultation | 20+ redundancies at one establishment in 90 days | Consultation starting 30 days before the first dismissal (45 days at 100+), with elected representatives |
+ | EU works councils | Varies by country | Information and consultation before the decision is announced — announcing first can invalidate the process |
+ Non-US employment is not at-will. In much of Europe and Latin America a reduction is a negotiated process with statutory notice and payments; assume weeks-to-months, not days, and budget accordingly.
+ ## Severance
+ Convention, not law (outside jurisdictions that mandate it): a floor of 4-8 weeks of base pay for ICs, plus roughly 1-2 weeks per year of service; 3-6 months for executives, usually written into their offer already. Common additions: health coverage continuation for the severance period, laptop, accelerated vesting or an extended option-exercise window (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance), and reference commitments.
+ Be more generous than the minimum by whatever you can afford. The delta is small against total burn, and it buys the two things the cut costs you: the departing employees' willingness to speak well of you, and the survivors' belief that this company handles people decently.
+ Severance is paid in exchange for a signed release. Do not hand out the money and ask for the release later, and do not use the release to buy silence about unlawful conduct — a non-disparagement clause that reaches whistleblowing or harassment is unenforceable in a growing list of jurisdictions and reads as evidence of something worse.
+ ## The Day
+ Choose the shape deliberately: a single all-hands announcement followed by individual conversations within the hour is the standard for a company under ~200. Larger, or multi-country, and you brief managers the night before with a script.
+ - Everyone learns the same day. A cut that leaks into a second day converts a hard morning into a week of paralysis.
+ - Individual conversations first for those leaving, then the all-hands for those staying — never the reverse, and never a group call for the departing.
+ - The conversation is 5-10 minutes: this is a decision, not a discussion; here is what happens to your pay, health coverage, and equity; here is who answers your questions and when the paperwork arrives. Do not explain the org theory to a person who just lost their job.
+ - The CEO is in the room. Delegating the announcement to HR is the single most-remembered failure of every badly run layoff.
+ - Access shutoff is timed to the conversation, not before it. Learning you are cut from a locked laptop is a story that outlives the company.
+ - Have the FAQ written before the day: severance details, health coverage, equity and exercise deadlines, references, immigration status for visa holders (a 60-day grace period is common in the US and is the most urgent question in the room), final paycheck timing, and what happens to their work.
+ ## Survivors
+ The layoff ends for the leavers on the day. For the people who stay it starts.
+ - Say the number and the plan: how many left, why this size, what the remaining team is expected to hit, and — the question everyone is holding — whether another cut is coming. Answer that one honestly, including "I cannot promise, and here is the number I am watching" (SKILL.md rule 4).
+ - Expect a productivity trough of weeks and voluntary attrition afterwards, weighted toward the people with the most options. Talk to your top performers individually within 48 hours, not with a retention bonus but with a role and a reason.
+ - Explicitly reassign the orphaned work. Anything not reassigned is quietly absorbed by the most conscientious person on the team, who then leaves.
+ - Kill projects, not just people. If the workload stays the same with fewer people, you did a burn exercise, not a strategy change.
+ - Do not backfill within two quarters. Hiring in the function you just cut tells the team the cut was arbitrary.
+ ## Traps
+ | Trap | Why it fails | Do instead |
+ | Two cuts in six months | Trust collapses; the best people leave between them | Size on the bear case once (SKILL.md rule 5) |
+ | Framing it as performance | Departing people hear a lie, survivors hear the criteria wrong | Say it is a plan change and that these roles are not in the new plan |
+ | Announcing before counsel reviewed the list | Disparate-impact exposure, blown notice periods, invalid releases | Legal review before any name is spoken aloud |
+ | Leaking to press before employees | Employees learn from a headline; you never recover the narrative | Sequence: individuals, all-hands, customers, investors, press — same day (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: communication) |
+ | Quiet rolling cuts to avoid a headline | Months of dread, the whole company job-hunting | One day, one number, one message |
+ | Cutting recruiting and keeping the hiring plan | The plan quietly becomes fiction | Rewrite the plan first; headcount follows the plan |

