+ # Memory Template — CEO
+ Create `~/Clawic/data/ceo/memory.md` with this structure:
+ ```markdown
+ # CEO Memory
+ ## Status
+ status: ongoing
+ last: YYYY-MM-DD
+ ## Context
+ <!-- Company, stage, model, headcount, where they are in the cycle -->
+ ## Decisions Made
+ <!-- Date, decision, owner, review date, falsifier — so nothing gets relitigated by accident -->
+ ## Open Threads
+ <!-- Live questions: the raise, the exec search, the pivot debate -->
+ ## Pain Points
+ <!-- What keeps coming back: board friction, co-founder tension, a metric that won't move -->
+ ## Preferences
+ <!-- Risk posture, transparency level, delegation surface, cadence, how blunt they want it -->
+ ---
+ *Updated: YYYY-MM-DD*
+ ```
+ Company facts the user declares (cash, burn, board roster, key accounts, red lines) belong in `~/Clawic/data/ceo/company.md`, not here: memory is what you observed, `company.md` and `config.yaml` are what they declared. An observation never overwrites a declared fact without asking.
+ ## Status Values
+ | Value | Meaning |
+ |-------|---------|
+ | `ongoing` | Still learning their company and how they decide |
+ | `complete` | Context is current; go straight to the recommendation |

### metrics.md  [docs]
+ # Metrics — What the CEO Actually Watches
+ A CEO needs five numbers they can recite cold and defend under hostile questioning, plus the discipline to keep everyone using the same definitions. Everything else is a dashboard someone else owns.
+ ## Pick The Model First
+ `business_model` (default b2b-saas) selects the row. Getting it wrong produces a dashboard that measures someone else's company.
+ | Business model | Primary health metric | The one that predicts it | Common vanity substitute |
+ |---|---|---|---|
+ | B2B SaaS | Net revenue retention | Logo retention by cohort, expansion rate | ARR growth with churn hidden inside |
+ | PLG / self-serve | Paid conversion of activated users | Activation rate (first value reached) | Signups |
+ | Marketplace | Liquidity: share of listings that transact in a window | Repeat rate on both sides | GMV |
+ | Consumer subscription | Retention at day 30/90/365 | Week-1 engagement depth | Downloads, MAU |
+ | Ecommerce / DTC | Contribution margin per order | Repeat purchase rate at 90 days | Revenue, ROAS alone |
+ | Services / agency | Utilization and realized rate | Pipeline coverage against booked capacity | Bookings |
+ | Hardware | Gross margin after warranty and returns | Sell-through, not sell-in | Units shipped |
+ | Anything pre-PMF | Retention curve shape (does any cohort flatten?) | Weekly customer conversations | Total users |
+ Default when unclear: the metric that, if it stalled for two quarters, would end the company. That is the one.
+ ## The Five
+ Keep exactly five on the CEO page, unchanged for at least a year unless the model changes:
+ 1. **Growth** — the revenue number for your model, monthly, with the rate not just the level.
+ 2. **Retention** — cohort-based, at the interval that matters for your model.
+ 3. **Cash** — balance, net burn, runway in months (canonical zones and math → SKILL.md Quick Reference: finance).
+ 4. **Efficiency** — one ratio: burn multiple (net burn / net new ARR, Sacks), CAC payback in months, or contribution margin.
+ 5. **The bet** — the single leading indicator of this year's strategic bet. It is the only one that changes with the plan.
+ Changing a metric mid-year requires the same ceremony as changing a priority: announce it, say why, and show both definitions side by side for a quarter.
+ ## Definitions Discipline
+ Most metric arguments are definition arguments wearing a costume.
+ - Every metric has one written definition, one owner, and one query. Store them where the whole company can read them; contradictions found in a board meeting are the expensive version.
+ - The recurring disputes worth settling in writing: what counts in ARR (annualized monthly? contracted? does usage overage count? do month-to-month contracts?), whether churn is logo or revenue and gross or net, what makes a user "active", whether pipeline is created-date or close-date weighted, when revenue is recognized versus collected.
+ - Board metrics and internal metrics use the same definitions. Two sets of books, even innocent ones, is how a CEO ends up defending a number they did not know was different.
+ - Averages hide the business. Any metric that matters gets a distribution or a cohort split at least quarterly — an average sales cycle of 60 days can be two segments at 20 and 140.
+ - Instrument before you launch. Reconstructing a cohort from logs six months later costs a week and produces a number nobody trusts.
+ ## Leading Versus Lagging
+ Revenue is lagging by the length of your sales cycle. If your cycle is 90 days, this quarter's revenue was decided last quarter, and managing it now is theater. Pair every lagging metric with the leading one you can still act on:
+ | Lagging | Leading, actionable now |
+ |---|---|
+ | Quarterly revenue | Qualified pipeline created this month, coverage ratio |
+ | Annual churn | Product usage decline in accounts, support ticket sentiment, exec-sponsor contact |
+ | Headcount plan attainment | Offer acceptance rate, time-to-first-onsite |
+ | NPS | Time-to-first-value for new customers |
+ | Burn | Committed spend and open reqs — both are cash already promised |
+ ## Operating Cadence
+ | Rhythm | Who | Content |
+ |---|---|---|
+ |---|---|---|
+ | Weekly | Exec team, 30 min | The five, versus plan, exceptions only; anything off-plan gets an owner and a date |
+ | Monthly | Company | Same five at the all-hands (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: communication); financials closed and reviewed with the finance owner |
+ | Quarterly | Board | The five plus the bet's progress, plan reforecast, one strategic question (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: board) |
+ | Annually | Company | Reset the bet and the plan (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: strategy) |
+ The weekly meeting reviews variance, not status. If the number is on plan, it takes ten seconds. A metric review where every number is explained at equal length teaches the team that reporting matters more than moving.
+ ## Reading Numbers Like a CEO
+ - A number that moved and nobody can explain is a broken pipeline until proven otherwise. Verify the instrumentation before you build a story on it.
+ - Two consecutive months is noise; three is a trend; one month is an anecdote with a decimal point.
+ - When a metric improves right after you started watching it, check what stopped being counted.
+ - Ask for the denominator every time. Conversion "up 40%" on a traffic drop is not a win.
+ - Segment before you conclude. Total churn flat can mean your best segment is growing while your largest is collapsing.
+ - Benchmarks are for calibration, never targets. Copying a public company's target rate at your stage imports the wrong constraint; your own trajectory is the comparison that matters.
+ ## Traps
+ | Trap | Why it fails | Do instead |
+ | Adding metrics after every bad quarter | The page becomes 30 numbers and the org loses focus | Five on the CEO page; the rest live with their owners |
+ | Board metrics ≠ internal metrics | You get surprised by your own reporting | One definition set, one source |
+ | Chasing a metric you cannot act on | Motion without leverage | Every metric on the page has a lever and an owner |
+ | Rewarding on a single metric | It gets gamed within a quarter, reliably | Pair every incentive metric with a quality guardrail |
+ | Reporting bookings as revenue | Cash arrives later or not at all; the plan is fiction | Say which is which every time (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: finance) |

### operations.md  [docs]
- # Executive Operations
- ## CEO Calendar
- The calendar is the strategy, observed. Porter & Nohria's HBR CEO time study found meetings consume roughly 70% of CEO hours — the default is drift; deep work exists only if defended.
- | Block | Rule |
- |-------|------|
- | Deep work (~4h/week minimum) | Strategy, writing, thinking. Scheduled first, defended like a board meeting |
- | 1:1s | Weekly with directs, monthly skip-levels (structure in `people.md`) |
- | External | Investors, customers, partners, recruiting — the parts only the CEO can do |
- | All-hands | Monthly minimum |
- | Board | Prep and meetings, monthly rhythm |
- | Buffer | Gaps between meetings; a packed calendar makes every overrun cascade |
- **Monthly calendar audit**: score the last 4 weeks against your 3 priorities. A priority with near-zero hours is a stated priority, not a real one — fix the calendar or fix the priority list.
- ## Executive Team Meetings
- Meetings are the medium of managerial work (Grove, *High Output Management*) — run them as production, not ceremony.
- **Weekly staff (60-90 min)**: metrics review (15) → decisions needed (30) → cross-functional blockers (15) → announcements (10). Decisions in the middle, when energy is highest; status that could be a document gets sent as a document.
- **Quarterly offsite (1-2 days)**: strategy review, team dynamics, next-quarter planning. No laptops — the point is the conversation the weekly meeting never has room for.
- ## Communication Rhythms
- | Audience | Cadence | Format |
- |----------|---------|--------|
- | Exec team | Weekly | Staff meeting |
- | All company | Weekly | Written update |
- | All company | Monthly | All-hands, live Q&A |
- | Board | Monthly | Written update |
- | Board | Quarterly | In-person meeting |
- | Investors | Monthly | Email update |
- Repeat the strategy until you are sick of saying it — that is roughly when the org has heard it once. A message delivered a single time was not communicated; it was mentioned.
- ## Hiring Executives
- 1. Scorecard first: outcomes for the first year, not activity lists (method in `people.md`).
- 2. Hire for the next 12-18 months of scale, not the end state — the exec who ran a 5,000-person org usually cannot run your 50-person one.
- 3. Source: portfolio referrals and personal network beat cold search; retained recruiters widen the top of funnel.
- 4. Backchannel references BEFORE final rounds, from people not on their list — after the team falls in love, references get rationalized away.
- 5. CEO closes personally: the close is where execs decide if you are worth the risk.
- **Red flags**: cannot articulate a past failure as their own; blames prior teams for misses; claims outcomes without naming who did the work; asks no hard questions about your company (a strong exec is diligencing you too).
- ## Firing Executives
- **When**: pattern of misses, not one bad quarter; values misalignment (not style difference); consistently negative team feedback; you are managing around them instead of through them — that last one means you already decided.
- **How**: act within days of deciding — every week of lingering costs credibility with the team that saw it first. Generous terms, fast exit, brief and respectful communication focused forward. The org reads the exit as a statement of your values; make it one you would sign.
- ## CEO Self-Management
- | Risk | Mitigation |
- |------|------------|
- | Burnout | Sleep, exercise, real vacations — scheduled, not aspirational |
- | Isolation | External confidants, CEO peer group; the exec team cannot be your therapist |
- | Decision fatigue | Kill trivial decisions with defaults and delegation; spend judgment where it compounds |
- | Echo chamber | Front-line exposure monthly; reward the person who brings the bad news |
- | Imposter syndrome | Normal. The job is hard for everyone doing it honestly |
- The team calibrates to your demeanor before your words: anxiety spreads, calm spreads. Panic privately, with your confidants — never on the all-hands.

### partnerships.md  [docs]
+ # Partnerships — Which Ones Are Real
+ Most partnerships produce a press release and nothing else. The base rate is bad enough that the default answer is no, and the burden of proof sits with the person proposing it.
+ ## The Four Tests Before Any Partnership
+ 1. **Whose number moves?** Name the person at the partner whose compensation depends on this working. No such person, no partnership — you have a logo swap.
+ 2. **Is it their top three?** A partnership that is your priority one and their priority eleven will be staffed accordingly. Ask what it displaces on their roadmap.
+ 3. **What does the first 10k of revenue look like, and by when?** If nobody can describe the first transaction concretely, there will not be one.
+ 4. **What does it cost us if it works?** Success has a price: engineering commitment, margin share, channel conflict with your direct sales, a dependency you cannot exit.
+ Kill criteria set in advance: a date and a number at which the partnership is wound down without a debate.
+ ## Types and Their Real Failure Modes
+ | Type | What you get | How it actually fails |
+ |---|---|---|
+ | Reseller / channel | Their sales force, their customer relationships | They sell what is easiest to sell; without enablement and margin, you are the third option in the bag |
+ | Referral | Warm leads at low cost | Nobody's job; volume decays to zero in two quarters unless a person owns it on both sides |
+ | Technology / integration | Retention lift, joint customers | Built, announced, then unmaintained — their API changes and nobody notices for a month |
+ | OEM / white label | Volume, deep revenue | You lose the end customer relationship, the brand, and eventually the pricing power |
+ | Marketplace listing | Distribution, procurement path | Listing is not distribution; ranking and co-selling incentives are, and both cost money |
+ | Co-marketing | Reach, credibility | Effort symmetric, benefit asymmetric toward the bigger brand |
+ | Strategic investment from a partner | Capital plus commitment | Signals exclusivity to their rivals, complicates your next round and any sale (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: exit) |
+ ## Terms That Decide the Outcome
+ - **Exclusivity**: never grant it without a floor — a minimum volume or revenue commitment that, if missed, converts the deal to non-exclusive automatically. Territory or vertical exclusivity is more survivable than product exclusivity. Exclusive means you cannot serve a customer who wants you directly; price that.
+ - **Term and exit**: 12 months with renewal by mutual agreement beats a 3-year deal with a termination clause you will not want to use. Include what happens to shared customers on exit — that is the clause you will need.
+ - **Margin and who owns the customer**: who bills, who supports, who holds the data, who renews. Ambiguity here is where partnerships turn adversarial.
+ - **Enablement obligations**: named people, training dates, deal registration. A partnership without deal registration produces channel conflict with your own reps within a quarter.
+ - **MFN and change of control**: a most-favored-nation clause constrains your pricing everywhere; a change-of-control clause in a partner contract can complicate or kill your acquisition (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance).
+ - **Support and SLA pass-through**: if they sell your SLA, you are on the hook for a promise their salesperson made.
+ ## Making One Work
+ 1. One owner on each side with the deal in their goals — CEO-to-CEO enthusiasm does not survive either party's next quarter.
+ 2. Start with a bounded pilot: 90 days, defined scope, one metric, both sides' effort written down.
+ 3. Land one joint customer before building anything general. The first real deal reveals whose commitment is real.
+ 4. Cadence: monthly working call at the operator level, quarterly at exec level. Skipped operator calls are the leading indicator of death.
+ 5. Announce after there is a customer, not before. A launch announcement is the traditional peak of most partnerships.
+ 6. Review at the pre-set date against the pre-set number, and wind down cleanly when it misses. Zombie partnerships consume enablement, support, and roadmap attention forever.
+ ## Channel Conflict
+ Decide the rules before the first conflict, in writing: which segments or geographies are direct, which are partner-led, how a contested account is resolved, and whether reps are paid on partner-sourced revenue. Reps paid nothing on partner deals will actively sabotage the channel, quietly and effectively.
+ ## The Big-Partner Asymmetry
+ When the partner is 100x your size: their legal process alone runs months, their champion will change roles mid-deal, and their standard contract will include terms (audit rights, MFN, IP licensing, unlimited liability) that a startup should not sign. Get a second champion before you invest, cap your liability, keep the term short, and never bet the roadmap on their timeline. The reputational lift is real — the revenue usually is not, and it arrives a year after the announcement.

### pe-backed.md  [docs]
+ # Sponsor-Backed CEO — PE Boards, Covenants, and the Clock
+ A private-equity-backed CEO is graded against a different scoreboard than a venture CEO, on a shorter clock, with a board that owns control rather than a minority stake. Advice that transfers from venture will get you fired in year two.
+ ## What Actually Differs
+ | Dimension | Venture-backed | Sponsor-backed |
+ |---|---|---|
+ | Scoreboard | Growth rate against the next round's bar | EBITDA growth and deleveraging against the value-creation plan |
+ | Board | Minority investors, advisory posture | Control owner; the board can replace you at will |
+ | Failure mode | Running out of cash | Breaching a covenant |
+ | Time horizon | Next round, 18-24 months | Hold period, typically 3-7 years, with an exit already imagined |
+ | Your equity | Founder shares | A management incentive plan, usually vesting on time plus a return hurdle |
+ | Reporting | Monthly update, quarterly board | Monthly pack on a deadline, weekly flash in the first 100 days, plus ad-hoc requests |
+ The single mental shift: the sponsor is not evaluating your company, they are evaluating their return on it. Every proposal lands better when it is expressed as its effect on EBITDA, cash conversion, or exit multiple.
+ ## The First 100 Days With a New Sponsor
+ - The value-creation plan exists before you were hired or re-signed. Get it in writing, with the underwriting assumptions — the model that justified the purchase price is the document you are actually being measured against.
+ - Reconcile it to reality immediately. If an assumption is wrong, say so in month one, when it is their model's error; in month nine it becomes your miss.
+ - Expect a 100-day plan with workstreams, owners, and weekly reporting. Staff it rather than resisting it; the reporting burden is real and permanent, and a CFO who can produce the pack on time is your most important hire (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: people).
+ - Establish which decisions need sponsor consent versus notification. Get the delegation of authority in writing early; discovering a consent right mid-deal is how CEOs lose credibility (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance).
+ ## Debt Is the Real Governor
+ Leveraged structures mean the balance sheet, not the market, sets the pace.
+ - Know your covenants precisely: the leverage ratio (net debt / EBITDA), fixed-charge or interest coverage, minimum liquidity, and the exact definition of "EBITDA" in the credit agreement — it is a negotiated definition, not the accounting one.
+ - Model headroom monthly on a rolling forward basis, not on the last-reported quarter. Covenant tests hit on trailing twelve-month figures, so a weak quarter enters the calculation and stays for a year.
+ - Approaching a breach: go to the lender early with a plan. Lenders forgive bad news delivered ahead of time and price late news punitively — waivers, amendments, and fees are available to a CEO who called in advance, and rarely to one who did not (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: crisis).
+ - Growth investments compete with debt service in a way they never did with venture money. Every spend proposal needs a payback period the sponsor recognizes, typically inside the hold.
+ ## Add-On M&A
+ Most sponsor theses include acquisitions to build scale and arbitrage the multiple (buy small at a lower multiple, exit combined at a higher one). You will be asked to source and integrate them.
+ - Integration capacity is the constraint, not deal flow. Two acquisitions a year is a lot for a company that has never done one (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: acquisitions).
+ - Quality of earnings work on the target is done by the sponsor's diligence provider; your job is the operational read they cannot do — whether the customers stay, whether the systems merge, whether the team is real.
+ - Synergy numbers in the model become your targets. Negotiate them before the deal closes, because you will be reported against them for the rest of the hold.
+ ## Your Own Economics
+ - The management incentive plan (MIP) is a pool of equity or options for the leadership team, commonly in the high single digits to low teens as a share of equity, allocated by the sponsor with CEO input. Ask three questions before you sign: what percentage is mine, what does it vest on (time, MOIC or IRR hurdles, or both), and what happens on a sale, a recapitalization, or my termination without cause.
+ - Understand leverage's effect on your outcome: in a levered structure the equity value is highly sensitive to the exit multiple and to debt paydown, so a modest EBITDA improvement can be worth a great deal — and a modest miss can wipe the equity out entirely.
+ - Rolling equity from a prior sale into the new deal is a real investment decision, not a loyalty gesture. Evaluate it as one.
+ - Get the terms reviewed by counsel who does MIPs. Terms differ enormously between sponsors and are rarely negotiable after signing.
+ ## Managing the Sponsor Relationship
+ - No surprises, at a higher frequency than a venture board: the deal partner should hear bad news by phone the day you know it (SKILL.md rule 6).
+ - Learn who actually decides. The deal partner, the operating partner, and the investment committee have different concerns; a proposal that survives all three is written for all three.
+ - Operating partners are a resource, not an inspection. Give them a real problem to own and the relationship improves permanently.
+ - The exit clock is always running. From roughly 18 months before the intended exit, everything you do is read through what it looks like in a data room: clean financials, contracted recurring revenue, low customer concentration (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: customers), documented processes, and a management team that is not just you (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: exit).
+ - If your read is that the plan cannot be hit, say it early with an alternative. Sponsors replace CEOs for missing quietly, far more often than for missing.

### people.md  [docs]
- # People and Culture
- ## CEO Time Allocation
- As a rule of thumb, roughly half a CEO's time goes to people — hiring, coaching, culture, exits. This is not overhead; it IS the job. Treat the split below as a target to defend against calendar drift, not a measured law.
- | Activity | Cadence | Purpose |
- |----------|---------|---------|
- | 1:1s with directs | Weekly | Alignment, coaching, blockers |
- | Skip levels | Monthly | Ground truth, culture pulse |
- | Recruiting | Aim ~20% of time in growth phases | Pipeline never stops |
- | Culture work | Ongoing | Modeling, reinforcing, correcting |
- ## Culture Definition
- Culture is not what you say. It's what you tolerate, reward, and punish.
- **Culture levers:**
- - Who you hire (values filter)
- - Who you fire (values enforcement)
- - Who you promote (values reward)
- - What you celebrate (values visibility)
- - What you ignore (values erosion)
- ### Culture Debt
- Like technical debt, culture debt compounds:
- - One bad hire tolerated → team learns you don't mean it
- - One missed value → threshold lowers
- - Clean it fast or pay exponentially later
- ## Hiring Framework
- ### Scorecard Method
- Before searching, define:
- 1. **Mission** — Why this role exists
- 2. **Outcomes** — 3-5 measurable results in first year
- 3. **Competencies** — Skills and behaviors needed
- 4. **Culture fit** — Which values this role must embody
- ### Interview Structure
- | Stage | Focus | Who |
- |-------|-------|-----|
- | Screen | Competency basics, motivation | Recruiter |
- | Deep dive | Past performance, specifics | Hiring manager |
- | Culture | Values alignment, work style | Cross-functional |
- | Reference | Verify claims, uncover risks | Hiring manager |
- | Close | Sell opportunity, negotiate | CEO (for execs) |
- ### Reference Calls
- Never skip. Always call people NOT on their list.
- **Questions that reveal:**
- - "If you were hiring for X role, would you hire this person?" (direct)
- - "What would their peers say about working with them?" (team fit)
- - "Where did they struggle?" (growth areas)
- - "Would you work with them again?" (ultimate test)
- ## Performance Management
- ### Continuous Feedback
- Annual reviews are too late. Weekly calibration prevents surprises.
- **1:1 structure:**
- 1. What's working? (2 min)
- 2. What's blocked? (5 min)
- 3. Where do you need help? (5 min)
- 4. Feedback from me (3 min)
- 5. Anything else? (5 min)
- ### Performance Categories
- | Category | % of Team | Action |
- |----------|-----------|--------|
- | Top performers | 10-20% | Stretch, retain, reward |
- | Strong performers | 60-70% | Develop, coach, clear path |
- | Underperformers | 10-20% | PIP or exit, don't wait |
- ### Managing Out
- When to act:
- - Pattern of misses, not one bad quarter
- - Values violation, regardless of performance
- - Role outgrew person, despite effort
- - Trust broken
- How to act:
- - Decide, then act fast — lingering destroys morale
- - Generous terms, clean exit
- - Protect their dignity publicly
- - Learn and adjust hiring criteria
- ## Compensation Philosophy
- ### Principles
- 1. **Pay market or above** — Cheap hires are expensive mistakes
- 2. **Internal equity matters** — Resentment kills teams
- 3. **Equity is alignment** — Skin in the game
- 4. **Transparency builds trust** — Explain the system
- ### Equity Guidelines
- *Ranges vary by role criticality and candidate strength.*
- ## Organizational Design
- ### Spans and Layers
- - Ideal span: 5-8 direct reports
- - More layers = slower communication
- - Fewer layers = overwhelmed managers
- ### When to Add a Layer
- | Signal | Meaning |
- |--------|---------|
- | Manager has 10+ directs | Span too wide |
- | Decisions take weeks | Communication bottleneck |
- | Manager in meetings all day | No time for work |
- | Team morale dropping | People feel unseen |
- ### Reorgs
- Reorg when:
- - Strategy changed and org doesn't match
- - Clear bottleneck everyone feels
- - Growth forced it (doubled headcount)
- Never reorg to:
- - Avoid a hard conversation
- - Accommodate one person
- - Look busy
- ### Signs of Healthy Exec Team
- - Debate ideas fiercely, commit fully
- - Solve problems together, not in silos
- - Call out each other's blind spots
- - Put company above function
- - Laugh together sometimes
- ### Signs of Dysfunction
- - Side conversations after meetings
- - Blame other teams for misses
- - CEO as tiebreaker for everything
- - Silence in meetings, politics outside
- - Competing for CEO attention
- ### Building Trust
- - Quarterly offsite (no laptops, real talk)
- - Vulnerability from the top
- - Shared wins and losses
- - No favorites, no backchannels
+ # People — Executives, Performance, Culture, Comp
+ Roughly half a CEO's time goes to people: hiring, coaching, culture, and exits. Treat that as a target to defend against calendar drift, not a measured law — and treat the exec team as the only team you actually manage.
+ ## Culture Is a Set of Decisions, Not a Document
+ Culture is what you tolerate, reward, and punish. The five levers, all of which the CEO controls at the top:
+ - **Who you hire** (the values filter) · **who you fire** (enforcement) · **who you promote** (the loudest signal, because it shows what actually gets rewarded) · **what you celebrate** (visibility) · **what you ignore** (erosion).
+ Culture debt compounds like technical debt: one tolerated bad hire teaches the team the values are decorative, and the next correction costs ten times more. The diagnostic question is always the same — whose behavior are you currently excusing because of their numbers? (SKILL.md rule 7)
+ Values are only useful when they cost something. A value that never caused you to reject a good candidate or exit a strong performer is a poster.
+ ## Hiring Executives
+ 1. **Scorecard before search**: mission of the role, 3-5 measurable outcomes for the first year, competencies, and the values it must embody. Written outcomes, not activity lists — "get to a repeatable enterprise motion with 6 reference customers" beats "build the sales org".
+ 2. **Hire for the next 12-18 months of scale, one stage ahead — not two.** The exec who ran a 5,000-person org usually cannot run your 50-person one; they will try to build the machine they are used to before there is anything to run (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: scaling).
+ 3. **Source**: portfolio and investor referrals plus your own network beat cold search; a retained recruiter widens the funnel and costs a percentage of first-year compensation. Your board should be producing candidates — ask each director for three names.
+ 4. **Interview for evidence, not narrative**: ask for a specific instance, then drill into what they personally did, what the numbers were before and after, and who else would confirm it. Strong operators get more specific under pressure; weak ones get more abstract.
+ 5. **Backchannel references before final rounds**, from people not on their list, including people who reported to them. After the team falls in love, references get rationalized away.
+ 6. **The CEO closes personally.** The close is where an exec decides whether you are worth the risk of their next five years.
+ **Red flags**: cannot name a failure as their own; blames prior teams; claims outcomes without naming who did the work; asks no hard questions about your business (a strong exec is diligencing you too); negotiates title harder than scope.
+ **The 90-day check on your own hire**: is the team better than before they arrived, and are they making decisions you would not have to make? If the answer is no at 90 days, it is usually no at 180.
+ ## Performance and Managing Out
+ Annual reviews are too late for a CEO's direct reports; the weekly 1:1 is the calibration mechanism, and no feedback should ever be new information in a review.
+ | Category | Share of team (typical) | Action |
+ |----------|------------------------|--------|
+ | Top performers | 10-20% | Stretch scope, retain deliberately, pay above band |
+ | Strong performers | 60-70% | Coach, clear path, keep engaged |
+ | Underperformers | 10-20% | Decide within a quarter: role change or exit |
+ Act when there is a pattern of misses (not one bad quarter), a values violation regardless of performance, the role has outgrown the person despite genuine effort, or trust is broken. The tell you have already decided: you are managing around them instead of through them.
+ Then act within days of deciding. Every week of lingering costs credibility with the team, who saw it before you did. Generous terms, clean exit, dignity preserved publicly, and a specific fix to your hiring criteria so you do not repeat it (exec-exit mechanics → SKILL.md Quick Reference: ceo-operating-system; a reduction rather than a performance exit → layoffs).
+ The exec you inherit or hire wrong costs 6-12 months of a function. That is the real price to weigh against the discomfort of the conversation.
+ ## Compensation
+ - Pay at market or above for the roles that compound; cheap senior hires are the most expensive mistake in this file.
+ - Internal equity beats external benchmarking when they conflict — resentment between peers destroys more value than a slightly high offer.
+ - Bands, written, with the logic explainable. People will compare, always; the only choice is whether your explanation exists before the comparison happens.
+ - Equity ranges by stage (convention; adjust for role criticality and candidate strength):
+ - Explain equity honestly: strike price, current 409A, what it is worth at three exit scenarios including zero, the 90-day exercise window and what it will cost them (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance). Founders who oversell equity create the bitterest alumni.
+ - Counteroffers to retain: they work for about six months and teach the whole team the mechanism for a raise. Fix the cause instead — usually scope, manager, or a band you never revisited.
+ **Healthy**: debate hard then commit fully; solve across functions rather than defending them; name each other's blind spots; the company outranks the function.
+ **Dysfunctional**: the real meeting happens after the meeting; misses get attributed to other teams; the CEO is the tiebreaker for everything; silence in the room and politics outside; execs competing for your attention rather than for outcomes.
+ - The CEO speaks last in debate and first in the announcement. Speaking first collapses the discussion into agreement with you (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: decisions).
+ - Never let an exec win by escalating to you what they should resolve with a peer. Send it back once, with a deadline, and only decide it if they return together.
+ - Quarterly offsite without laptops. The point is the conversation the weekly meeting has no room for.
+ - Founders without a functional role are the most common source of exec dysfunction: an unassigned founder becomes a floating opinion that undermines the person who does own the domain (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: cofounders).
+ ## Organization Design
+ - Span: 5-8 direct reports for managers of ICs, 4-6 for managers of managers (what breaks at each headcount threshold → SKILL.md Quick Reference: scaling).
+ - Reorg when strategy changed and the org no longer matches it, when a bottleneck everyone feels has a structural cause, or when growth genuinely forced it. Never to avoid a hard conversation, to accommodate one person, or to look decisive.
+ - Announce a reorg with the new decision rights, not just the new boxes. People care about who decides what, and a chart without that is a rumor generator (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: communication).
+ - Reorg cost is a full quarter of partial productivity. Two in a year and the org stops investing in any structure at all.

### pivot.md  [docs]
+ # Pivot — Persevere, Change, or Stop
+ Most companies do not die from picking the wrong direction; they die from taking eighteen months to admit it and then having no runway to act on the admission.
+ ## Decide Whether You Have Evidence or Fatigue
+ Pivot on falsified assumptions, not on a bad quarter or a founder's boredom. Write the assumption that has been falsified and the observation that falsified it. If you cannot write the sentence, you have fatigue, and the answer is a plan change, not a pivot.
+ | Signal | Reads as | Test |
+ |---|---|---|
+ |---|---|---|
+ | Retention curve decays to zero in every cohort | No value delivered; nothing to build on | Do any cohorts flatten? A flattening cohort is a segment, and a segment is a business |
+ | Growth only where you personally sell | Founder charisma, not product pull | Hand three deals to someone else; do they close? |
+ | Customers renew but nobody expands | A feature, not a platform | Is the ceiling the segment, the pricing, or the product (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: pricing)? |
+ | Sales cycle keeps stretching quarter over quarter | Budget owner does not feel the pain | Ask who gets fired if this problem persists. If nobody, there is no budget |
+ | Usage strong, willingness to pay zero | Value real, capture wrong | Try the price change before the pivot — it is reversible and takes weeks |
+ | The team keeps proposing new markets | You are already pivoting, informally | Force the choice into the open before it fragments the roadmap |
+ | Nobody can name what you learned this quarter | Not iterating — waiting | Set a pre-committed decision date, now |
+ **Pre-commit the criteria before the experiment**, not after: the metric, the threshold, and the date. "If paid conversion is under 3% by March 31 with 200 signups, we change segments." Criteria written after the data is in are always met.
+ ## The Pivot Types (Ries)
+ Naming the type prevents the most expensive version — changing everything at once and learning nothing.
+ | Type | You keep | You change |
+ | Zoom-in | One feature everyone uses | The rest of the product becomes the product |
+ | Zoom-out | The whole product | It becomes a feature of something larger |
+ | Customer segment | The product | Who you sell it to |
+ | Customer need | The customer | The problem you solve for them |
+ | Platform | The technology | App becomes platform, or the reverse |
+ | Business architecture | Capability | High-margin/low-volume ↔ low-margin/high-volume |
+ | Value capture | Everything | How you monetize (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: pricing) |
+ | Engine of growth | Product | Viral, paid, or sticky loop |
+ | Channel | Product | How it reaches the buyer |
+ | Technology | Problem and customer | How you solve it — usually cost or performance |
+ Rule: change one axis at a time where the runway allows it. Two axes at once means a failure teaches you nothing about which one was wrong. The exception is a genuine restart, where pretending otherwise wastes months.
+ ## Can You Afford It
+ - Budget 6-12 months from decision to a new signal, and more for enterprise sales cycles. Enter with at least months-to-signal + 6 of runway at the post-pivot burn, or the pivot is really a fundraising plan with a story attached (SKILL.md rule 4).
+ - If runway is short, the sequence inverts: cut first to buy the time, then pivot (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: layoffs). Pivoting at 5 months of cash produces a half-executed idea and a desperate raise.
+ - Cash and team are the two assets that survive a pivot. Protect both in the sizing; a pivot that loses the three people who could execute it is a shutdown with extra steps (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: shutdown).
+ ## Executing It
+ 1. Decide with a pre-mortem and one named decision-maker (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: decisions). A pivot decided by consensus gets relitigated at the first bad month.
+ 2. Pre-wire the board individually before any deck exists. You want the objections while the plan is still editable (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: board).
+ 3. Write the memo: what we believed, what we observed, what we now believe, what we will do for the next two quarters, what would prove us wrong. Two pages, no hedging.
+ 4. Announce internally in one meeting, with the memo. Frame it as a conclusion drawn from data, not a confession — conviction is what the team is deciding whether to follow (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: communication).
+ 5. Kill the old thing on a date, in writing. A pivot with the old product still on life support splits the team's attention and both efforts starve.
+ 6. Expect and accept departures: people joined the old mission. Talk to your key people individually within 48 hours, before they hear it in a group.
+ 7. Existing customers get a support commitment with an end date, an honest migration story, or a refund. What you do here is the reference call for your next company.
+ 8. Set the next decision date the same day you announce. A pivot with no review date is how a company arrives at the same problem twice.
+ ## Anti-Patterns
+ - **The zombie pivot**: new pitch, same product, same customers. Only the deck changed.
+ - **The serial pivot**: three directions in a year. The team stops believing any of them, and investors read velocity as panic — after the second, the market's read is that the founders cannot choose.
+ - **Pivoting away from the hard part**: switching to a market where you have no advantage because the current one is difficult. Where-to-play needs a how-to-win (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: strategy).
+ - **The quiet pivot**: strategy changes but nobody tells the team, so half the roadmap still serves the old thesis.
+ - **Pivoting on the loudest customer**: one prospect's promise is not a segment (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: customers).
+ - **Sunk-cost persistence**: "we've spent two years on this" is an argument for stopping, not continuing (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: decisions).
+ ## When The Answer Is Stop, Not Pivot
+ No falsifiable new thesis, no cash to test one, and no team that wants to — that is not a pivot candidate. Returning remaining capital beats spending it on a direction nobody believes in, and it is the version of this that investors remember well (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: shutdown).

### pricing.md  [docs]
+ # Pricing — The CEO's Highest-Leverage Lever
+ Price flows straight to gross profit: a 1% price increase held with no volume loss is a 1% revenue gain at ~100% margin, while a 1% volume gain costs whatever it costs to acquire. It is also the decision most companies make once, by intuition, and never revisit.
+ ## What Only The CEO Can Decide
+ - The pricing **metric** (what a customer buys a unit of) — seats, usage, outcomes, sites, transactions.
+ - The **packaging** (what is in which tier and what is deliberately withheld).
+ - The **discount authority** matrix and who can break it.
+ - Whether you are the premium option or the cheap one. Both work; being accidentally in the middle does not.
+ Everything downstream — page layout, promo calendar, sales enablement — belongs to `cmo` and sales.
+ ## Choosing the Metric
+ The pricing metric should rise with the value the customer receives and be predictable enough to budget. Test any candidate against four questions:
+ 1. Does it grow when their success grows? (Seats do not, if your product removes headcount.)
+ 2. Can the buyer predict next year's bill within ~20%? Unpredictable bills lose enterprise renewals regardless of value.
+ 3. Can they game it cheaply? (Shared logins mean seat pricing is really site pricing.)
+ 4. Can you measure it defensibly, and would you show them the meter?
+ Usage pricing aligns with value and terrifies procurement; seat pricing is legible and caps you; hybrid (platform fee + usage) is the common resolution — commit floor plus overage. Pick one primary metric; two independent meters make your pricing unexplainable in a sales call, which is the real cost.
+ ## Finding the Level
+ - Willingness to pay is research, not a guess (Ramanujam & Tacke, *Monetizing Innovation*): ask about price before you build the packaging, not after the roadmap is locked.
+ - Van Westendorp's four questions ("at what price is it too expensive / expensive but worth it / a bargain / so cheap you'd doubt quality") gives a defensible range from ~20-30 interviews with real buyers. It gives a range, never a number.
+ - The most reliable signal is behavioral: what they pay today for the alternative, including the internal spreadsheet and the person maintaining it.
+ - The value-based sanity check: quantify the annual value delivered per customer, then price at 10-25% of it. Below 10% you are leaving money on the table; at 25% or above you need proof of value most buyers will not have. The bands cover the whole range: <10 under-priced, 10-25 defensible, ≥25 proof-dependent.
+ - If nobody ever complains about price, you are under-priced. Convention worth holding: if you win more than about 80% of deals you compete in, you are competing on price, not value.
+ ## Raising Prices
+ The right cadence is annual and expected, not heroic and rare.
+ 1. Raise on new customers first. Watch win rate and sales-cycle length for one full cycle before touching the base.
+ 2. Grandfather existing customers for a defined period — commonly one renewal cycle — and say the period out loud so it is a decision, not a permanent exception.
+ 3. Give 60-90 days' notice to existing customers, in a message that leads with what they get, then states the change and the date (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: communication).
+ 4. Expect and budget for churn among the least-engaged customers. The arithmetic: a 15% price increase with 5% churn is a net ~9% revenue gain, and you shed your worst accounts. Compute it with your own numbers before deciding: net change = (1 + increase) × (1 − churn) − 1.
+ 5. Never raise price in the same quarter as a reliability incident or a shipped regression.
+ 6. Handle escalations with a fixed policy decided in advance (who gets an exception and for how long), or the loudest customers set your price.
+ ## Discount Discipline
+ - Discounts buy something or they are a gift: annual prepay, multi-year commit, a case study, a reference, a bigger scope. Write the exchange into the deal.
+ - Authority ladder, set by `discount_authority` (default rep:10, vp:20, ceo:above) — and approvals above the top rung get logged and reviewed monthly. Realized price (revenue ÷ list value) is the metric; if average realized price is falling quarter over quarter, list price is fiction.
+ - Never discount to close inside the quarter. Buyers learn the calendar in one cycle and every deal moves to the last week.
+ - End-of-life discounting on a legacy plan is a pricing decision, not a sales one — it sets the floor for renewals across the base.
+ - Price cuts are near-irreversible and public ones reset market expectations permanently; concede term, scope, or volume first (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: competition).
+ ## Packaging
+ - Three tiers is the default: entry that removes the objection, core where you want most customers, premium that makes core look reasonable and captures enterprise budget.
+ - Differentiate tiers by dimensions of value (scale, security, support, controls), never by artificially crippling the core workflow — a hobbled product reads as punishment.
+ - The features that belong in the enterprise tier are the ones bought by people who are not users: SSO, audit logs, permissions, data residency, an SLA, procurement paperwork.
+ - Free plan versus free trial: free plans work when usage creates value for other users or when your marginal cost is near zero; trials work when time-to-value is under the trial length. Running both usually means neither is designed.
+ - Every added tier or add-on costs sales-conversation clarity. If a rep cannot explain the lineup in 30 seconds, buyers cannot either.
+ ## Traps
+ | Trap | Why it fails | Do instead |
+ |---|---|---|
+ | Cost-plus pricing | Your costs are irrelevant to the buyer's value | Value-based, sanity-checked against alternatives |
+ | Copying a competitor's page | Imports their cost structure, segment, and mistakes | Price to your own value and segment |
+ | Pricing for the biggest logo you want | Whole book anchored to one deal that may never close | Price for the segment you actually serve |
+ | Never revisiting price | You give away every product improvement for free | Annual review on the calendar, with the data |
+ | Custom pricing for everyone | No comparability, no forecast, renewals negotiated from zero | Published tiers with a defined enterprise path |
+ | Announcing a rise without a value story | Reads as extraction; churn concentrates in your advocates | Ship visible value first, then raise |

### scaling.md  [docs]
+ # Scaling — What Breaks at Each Headcount Threshold
+ Companies do not degrade smoothly. They work, then break at a size, then work again once the CEO installs the thing that stage requires. The failure is always the same: installing it a year after the pain, having spent that year believing the problem was individual people.
+ ## The Breakpoints
+ | Size | What breaks | What to install | CEO's job changes to |
+ |---|---|---|---|
+ | 1-10 | Nothing yet; the room knows everything | Weekly written priorities, one metric | Doing the work, selling personally |
+ | 10-25 | Everyone-in-every-decision stops fitting; the CEO becomes the approval queue | Named owners per area, one decision doc, a real 1:1 cadence | Deciding what NOT to decide (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: decisions) |
+ | 25-50 | Informal communication fails: two people confidently give a customer different answers | First managers (promoted with training, not just title), written strategy, planning cadence | Managing managers, hiring the first execs |
+ | 50-150 | Nobody knows everyone; new hires stop absorbing culture by osmosis; recruiting quality slips | Levels and comp bands, onboarding, explicit values with examples, a real interview loop | Org design, exec team as a team |
+ | 150-500 | Dunbar's ceiling: coordination cost dominates; politics appears; local optimization | Formal planning, internal comms function, second-line managers, clear interfaces between teams | Choosing the three things, defending focus |
+ | 500+ | Strategy travels through too many translations to survive | Written strategy repeated relentlessly, leadership development, succession bench (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: succession) | Capital allocation, leadership of leaders |
+ Hoffman's blitzscaling stages are 10x jumps — family (<10), tribe (10-99), village (100-999), city (1,000-9,999), nation (10,000+) — each needing a different management model. The thresholds in the table above are finer than that on purpose: the pain arrives well before the next 10x, and it is the pain, not the stage label, that tells you what to install. Plan the install for the next threshold while you are at 60% of it — new structure takes a quarter to work, and installing it during the pain guarantees the pain lasts a quarter longer.
+ ## Signals You Passed a Threshold
+ - Decisions wait for you when you are on a plane.
+ - Two teams built the same thing, and both were right to think nobody else was.
+ - New hires take longer to become productive than the last cohort did.
+ - Your best people are spending their time coordinating, not producing.
+ - You hear about problems from customers before you hear them internally.
+ - Meetings grow attendees without adding decisions.
+ - People ask "who owns this?" and get three answers.
+ Any two of these at once means the structure is a stage behind. Diagnose the stage, not the person who dropped the ball.
+ ## Hiring Ahead — By Exactly One Stage
+ Hire people who have operated at your next stage, not two beyond. The exec who ran a 5,000-person org usually cannot run your 50-person one: they are used to a machine you do not have and will try to build it before there is anything to run.
+ - The test: ask what they would personally do in their first 60 days here. Answers that begin with hiring a team are a stage-mismatch signal.
+ - Hire for the next 12-18 months of scale. Beyond that you are paying now for a company that may not exist.
+ - Growth outpacing the team is normal; the failure is refusing to say so. Every scaling company has people who were great at 20 and are struggling at 100 — that is a role conversation, not a character verdict (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: people).
+ ## Manager Math
+ - Span 5-8 direct reports for managers of ICs; 4-6 for managers of managers, because coaching a manager costs more per head.
+ - Layers: every layer adds a translation of strategy and roughly a week to any cross-team decision. Under 150 people, three layers is enough (CEO → manager → IC, with function heads); a fourth before then is a symptom of title inflation.
+ - Adding a layer is justified by span or complexity, never by giving someone a promotion. The alternative promotion path (senior IC ladder) has to exist before the pressure arrives, or every good engineer becomes a mediocre manager.
+ - Promoting your best IC into management without training loses you an excellent IC and produces a struggling manager. Budget training and a probation understanding, both directions.
+ ## Process Without Bureaucracy
+ Add process when the failure has occurred twice and cost something both times. Each new process needs an owner, a written trigger ("when X happens, do Y"), and a review date at which it gets deleted if it is not earning its cost.
+ - Good process removes decisions from the queue. Bad process adds approvals to it — measure by whether the process makes decisions faster at the edge.
+ - Kill one process for every two you add, or you will arrive at 200 people with a change-advisory board and no memory of why.
+ - Onboarding is the highest-return process at every stage above 25: time-to-first-contribution is the metric. Measure it per cohort against your own previous cohort, never a benchmark; unowned, it drifts up quietly, and the first evidence is usually a new hire's third week with nothing shipped.
+ ## Scaling Yourself
+ The CEO's job description is rewritten at each threshold, and most CEOs keep doing the previous version because they are good at it. Each quarter, name the thing you did last quarter that someone else should now own, hand it over with the context and the authority, and accept it being done at 80% of your standard — at scale, 80% done by an owner beats 100% done by a bottleneck (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: ceo-operating-system).
+ Personal breaking point comes with the same signals as the org's: your calendar is full of things only you can do because you never made anyone else able to do them. That is not commitment, it is a design error.

### setup.md  [docs]
+ # Setup — CEO
+ Read this on first use to load user preferences. Do not interview the user.
+ ## Your Attitude
+ You are in the seat with them. Direct, numerate, unsentimental about ideas and careful with people. You give the recommendation before the reasoning, name the trade-off you accepted, and say when the honest answer is "there is no good option, here is the least bad one."
+ ## How To Load Preferences
+ 1. Read `~/Clawic/data/ceo/config.yaml` if it exists. Apply its values.
+ 2. For anything absent, use the defaults in the Configuration table of `SKILL.md` — do not ask.
+    - `mode: advise`, `stage: seed`, `funding_model: vc`, `runway_alarm_months: 12`, `currency: USD`, `fiscal_year_start: January`, `planning_framework: OKR`, `board_cadence: quarterly`, `deliverable_style: memo`, `voice_file: none`.
+ 3. Read `~/Clawic/data/ceo/company.md` for declared company facts (stage, cash, burn, board roster, key accounts, red lines) and `~/Clawic/data/ceo/memory.md` for prior context. Absence is fine; proceed without comment.
+ 4. Falling back to defaults changes the answer: at `stage: seed` the advice assumes founder-led sales and a 3-seat board. If the user's first message contradicts a default, use their fact and record it — do not argue from the default.
+ Work from defaults immediately. Never open with questions about their company, priorities, or how proactive to be.
+ ## Recording Preferences (only when the user declares one)
+ Write to config, company, or memory **only** when the user states something in the course of the work — never as a preflight questionnaire.
+ - User names their stage, funding model, currency, fiscal year, planning framework, board cadence, or preferred deliverable format → update the matching key in `config.yaml`.
+ - User states company facts (cash on hand, burn, headcount, board members, biggest customers, investors, things they will never do) → record in `~/Clawic/data/ceo/company.md`; that file is the context every recommendation should be computed from.
+ - User expresses a stance (risk posture, transparency level, what they refuse to delegate, cadence they keep) → record it under the matching preference area in `~/Clawic/data/ceo/memory.md`.
+ - User corrects earlier guidance → update the stored value so you don't repeat it.
+ If the user has said nothing, store nothing. One question maximum, and only when a decision is blocked and no default can resolve it — cash on hand and burn are the usual blockers.
+ ## What Memory Holds
+ The file format is in the memory template (→ SKILL.md, first paragraph). Track their operating context, the decisions already made (so you don't relitigate them), recurring pain points, and how they like to be pushed — but only from what they actually reveal.

### shutdown.md  [docs]
+ # Shutdown — Winding Down Well
+ Nobody plans this and everyone does it badly the first time. The two decisions that determine whether it goes well are made months apart: deciding early enough to have cash for the wind-down, and paying the right people in the right order.
+ ## Decide While You Still Have Cash
+ Winding down costs money — final payroll, accrued PTO, severance, legal and accounting fees, contract terminations, lease settlement, tax filings, and the months of administration after the doors close. Reserve for it before it becomes unaffordable; a company that hits zero cash has lost every option except the worst one.
+ Signals it is time, taken together rather than singly: no falsifiable new thesis and no cash to test one (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: pivot); insiders declining to bridge after saying no twice (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: fundraising); the team's best people gone or going; and a market answer that has been consistent for four quarters.
+ The honorable version of this decision is returning remaining capital rather than spending it on a direction nobody believes in. Investors remember this precisely, and it is the single strongest reference you can carry into your next company.
+ ## Priority of Payments
+ Get this order right; it is where personal liability lives.
+ 1. **Payroll, including accrued PTO where the jurisdiction requires it, and payroll taxes.** Unremitted payroll taxes withheld from employees can attach personally to officers and responsible persons (in the US, the trust-fund recovery penalty), and no bankruptcy discharges that. Never fund anything else ahead of them.
+ 2. **Secured creditors** — venture debt, equipment financing, anyone with a lien on assets.
+ 3. **Priority unsecured claims** as defined by the applicable regime (some employee claims, certain taxes).
+ 4. **General unsecured creditors** — vendors, landlord, contractors.
+ 5. **Preferred equity** by preference stack order.
+ 6. **Common equity** — in practice, almost never anything.
+ Paying a friendly vendor or an insider ahead of this order near insolvency is a preference that can be clawed back and can become a personal claim against directors. Once insolvency is in view, decisions are judged by creditors, so document the reasoning for each one (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance).
+ ## Choosing the Mechanism
+ | Path | When it fits | Reality |
+ |---|---|---|
+ | **Solvent dissolution** | Enough cash to pay everyone and distribute the remainder | Cleanest; board and stockholder approval, filings, notice to creditors, final tax returns |
+ | **Assignment for the benefit of creditors (ABC)** | Insolvent, some assets, want speed and privacy (US) | Assignee takes control and liquidates; faster and cheaper than bankruptcy, and the common venture-backed route |
+ | **Chapter 7** | Insolvent, contested claims, need the court's protection | Trustee controls everything; slower, public, more expensive |
+ | **Chapter 11 / restructuring** | A viable business with an unviable balance sheet | Rarely worth it below substantial scale — the professional fees eat the company |
+ | **Acquihire / soft landing** | The team is the asset and someone wants it | Preferred outcome; run it while people are still there (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: exit) |
+ | **Non-US** | Varies widely | Many jurisdictions impose a duty to file for insolvency within a short statutory window once insolvent, with personal liability for directors who trade on. Get local counsel the week you see it coming |
+ Talk to counsel experienced in wind-downs before choosing. This is the single decision where cheap advice is most expensive.
+ ## The Sequence
+ 1. **Board approves** the wind-down and the plan, with the record of the deliberation. Notify your D&O insurer and confirm run-off coverage — claims arrive after the company is gone (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: governance).
+ 2. **Model the wind-down budget** to the last dollar: payroll to the end date, severance, PTO payouts, notice-period obligations, legal, accounting, insurance run-off, lease and vendor settlements.
+ 3. **Notice obligations**: WARN and its state equivalents apply to closings, not just layoffs (thresholds → SKILL.md Quick Reference: layoffs). Employment terminations in non-US jurisdictions carry statutory notice you must fund.
+ 4. **Tell the team in person, all at once**, with the date, their final pay, health coverage end, PTO payout, equity reality (state plainly that the options are almost certainly worthless — vague hope costs people money in exercise fees), and what help you will give. Then spend real effort on placing them: intro lists, a shared candidate doc, calls to peer CEOs. This is the part people remember for a decade.
+ 5. **Customers** get as much notice as you can afford, a data export, and where possible a migration path or an introduction to an alternative. Data deletion commitments must actually be honored; obligations survive the company.
+ 6. **Sell the assets**: IP, domain, code, brand, customer contracts (subject to assignment clauses), and hardware. Something is usually worth something, and it goes to creditors.
+ 7. **Investors** hear before the announcement, individually, from you. Include what remains and what you plan to return.
+ 8. **Close it properly**: final tax returns, payroll accounts, state registrations in every jurisdiction where you registered, cancel subscriptions and the registered agent last. An entity left half-dissolved keeps generating franchise tax and penalties for years.
+ ## What Not To Do
+ - Do not keep going on personal credit cards or founder loans hoping for a miracle; you convert a company failure into a personal one.
+ - Do not miss payroll to buy two more weeks. It is the one irreversible mistake in this file.
+ - Do not let people find out by having their access shut off, or from a press piece.
+ - Do not go silent with investors. The wind-down conversation is uncomfortable for one week; the reputation for disappearing lasts a career.
+ - Do not promise employees the equity might still be worth something.
+ - Do not skip the write-up. A short, honest post-mortem — what you believed, what was true, what you would do differently — is the thing that gets you the next round of trust (→ SKILL.md Quick Reference: decisions).

